2025-11-05 14:19:36 [simon@midnightblue] make sloppyhost an example for integrating fountain into an existing server
2025-11-05 14:20:52 [kimi-k2-0905] Below is a minimal, drop-in pattern you can paste into any Deno server to expose Fountain’s “slop”
2025-11-05 14:20:52 [kimi-k2-0905] endpoint without touching the rest of your stack.
2025-11-05 14:20:52 [kimi-k2-0905] It keeps the original sloppyhost.ts behaviour (NDJSON over TCP 8081) but adds two tiny helpers so
2025-11-05 14:20:52 [kimi-k2-0905] your existing HTTP routes can push messages into Fountain and read back whatever Fountain
2025-11-05 14:20:52 [kimi-k2-0905] broadcasts.
2025-11-05 14:20:52 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-05 14:20:52 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-05 14:20:52 [kimi-k2-0905] // fountain-bridge.ts
2025-11-05 14:20:52 [kimi-k2-0905] // Add this file to your project, import `attachFountain` once, done.
2025-11-05 14:20:52 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-05 14:20:52 [kimi-k2-0905] import { serve } from "https://deno.land/std@0.224.0/http/server.ts";
2025-11-05 14:20:52 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-05 14:20:52 [kimi-k2-0905] // ---------- 1.  TCP side (unchanged Fountain protocol) ----------
2025-11-05 14:20:52 [kimi-k2-0905] const encoder = new TextEncoder();
2025-11-05 14:20:52 [kimi-k2-0905] const decoder = new TextDecoder();
2025-11-05 14:20:52 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-05 14:20:52 [kimi-k2-0905] let fountainConn: Deno.Conn | null = null;
2025-11-05 14:20:52 [kimi-k2-0905] async function connectFountain() {
2025-11-05 14:20:52 [kimi-k2-0905]   try {
2025-11-05 14:20:52 [kimi-k2-0905]     fountainConn = await Deno.connect({ hostname: "localhost", port: 8081 });
2025-11-05 14:20:52 [kimi-k2-0905]     fountainConn.setNoDelay(true);
2025-11-05 14:20:52 [kimi-k2-0905]     readLoop();               // fire-and-forget reader
2025-11-05 14:20:52 [kimi-k2-0905]   } catch {
2025-11-05 14:20:52 [kimi-k2-0905]     console.warn("[bridge] Fountain not ready – retrying in 3 s");
2025-11-05 14:20:52 [kimi-k2-0905]     setTimeout(connectFountain, 3000);
2025-11-05 14:20:52 [kimi-k2-0905]   }
2025-11-05 14:20:52 [kimi-k2-0905] }
2025-11-05 14:20:52 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-05 14:20:52 [kimi-k2-0905] const rxBuf = new Uint8Array(1 << 20);
2025-11-05 14:20:52 [kimi-k2-0905] let rxTail = "";
2025-11-05 14:20:52 [kimi-k2-0905] async function readLoop() {
2025-11-05 14:20:52 [kimi-k2-0905]   while (fountainConn) {
2025-11-05 14:20:52 [kimi-k2-0905]     const n = await fountainConn.read(rxBuf);
2025-11-05 14:20:52 [kimi-k2-0905]     if (!n) { fountainConn.close(); fountainConn = null; connectFountain(); return; }
2025-11-05 14:20:52 [kimi-k2-0905]     rxTail += decoder.decode(rxBuf.subarray(0, n));
2025-11-05 14:20:52 [kimi-k2-0905]     let pos;
2025-11-05 14:20:52 [kimi-k2-0905]     while ((pos = rxTail.indexOf("\n")) >= 0) {
2025-11-05 14:20:52 [kimi-k2-0905]       const line = rxTail.slice(0, pos);
2025-11-05 14:20:52 [kimi-k2-0905]       rxTail = rxTail.slice(pos + 1);
2025-11-05 14:20:52 [kimi-k2-0905]       try {
2025-11-05 14:20:52 [kimi-k2-0905]         const msg = JSON.parse(line);
2025-11-05 14:20:52 [kimi-k2-0905]         onFountainMessage(msg);          // <-- your handler
2025-11-05 14:20:52 [kimi-k2-0905]       } catch {}
2025-11-05 14:20:52 [kimi-k2-0905]     }
2025-11-05 14:20:52 [kimi-k2-0905]   }
2025-11-05 14:20:52 [kimi-k2-0905] }
2025-11-05 14:20:52 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-05 14:20:52 [kimi-k2-0905] function onFountainMessage(msg: any) {
2025-11-05 14:20:52 [kimi-k2-0905]   // Whatever you want to do with Fountain traffic (log, forward to WS, etc.)
2025-11-05 14:20:52 [kimi-k2-0905]   console.log("[bridge] Fountain → ", msg);
2025-11-05 14:20:52 [kimi-k2-0905] }
2025-11-05 14:20:52 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-05 14:20:52 [kimi-k2-0905] export async function writeFountain(obj: unknown) {
2025-11-05 14:20:52 [kimi-k2-0905]   if (!fountainConn) return;
2025-11-05 14:20:52 [kimi-k2-0905]   try {
2025-11-05 14:20:52 [kimi-k2-0905]     const buf = encoder.encode(JSON.stringify(obj) + "\n");
2025-11-05 14:20:52 [kimi-k2-0905]     await fountainConn.write(buf);
2025-11-05 14:20:52 [kimi-k2-0905]   } catch { fountainConn = null; connectFountain(); }
2025-11-05 14:20:52 [kimi-k2-0905] }
2025-11-05 14:20:52 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-05 14:20:52 [kimi-k2-0905] // ---------- 2.  HTTP helpers for your existing server ----------
2025-11-05 14:20:52 [kimi-k2-0905] export function attachFountain(router: (req: Request) => Response | Promise<Response>) {
2025-11-05 14:20:52 [kimi-k2-0905]   connectFountain();                       // start background TCP client
2025-11-05 14:20:52 [kimi-k2-0905]   return (req: Request) => {
2025-11-05 14:20:52 [kimi-k2-0905]     const url = new URL(req.url);
2025-11-05 14:20:52 [kimi-k2-0905]     // POST /api/fountain  {message:"hello",from:"web"}
2025-11-05 14:20:52 [kimi-k2-0905]     if (req.method === "POST" && url.pathname === "/api/fountain") {
2025-11-05 14:20:52 [kimi-k2-0905]       return req.json().then((body: any) => {
2025-11-05 14:20:52 [kimi-k2-0905]         writeFountain(body);
2025-11-05 14:20:52 [kimi-k2-0905]         return new Response("ok", { status: 200 });
2025-11-05 14:20:52 [kimi-k2-0905]       });
2025-11-05 14:20:52 [kimi-k2-0905]     }
2025-11-05 14:20:52 [kimi-k2-0905]     // Everything else falls through to your normal router
2025-11-05 14:20:52 [kimi-k2-0905]     return router(req);
2025-11-05 14:20:52 [kimi-k2-0905]   };
2025-11-05 14:20:52 [kimi-k2-0905] }
2025-11-05 14:20:52 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-05 14:20:52 [kimi-k2-0905] // ---------- 3.  Example usage ----------
2025-11-05 14:20:52 [kimi-k2-0905] if (import.meta.main) {
2025-11-05 14:20:52 [kimi-k2-0905]   // Your existing router
2025-11-05 14:20:53 [kimi-k2-0905]   function myApp(req: Request) {
2025-11-05 14:20:53 [kimi-k2-0905]     if (req.method === "GET" && new URL(req.url).pathname === "/")
2025-11-05 14:20:53 [kimi-k2-0905]       return new Response("Hello from your real server");
2025-11-05 14:20:53 [kimi-k2-0905]     return new Response("Not Found", { status: 404 });
2025-11-05 14:20:53 [kimi-k2-0905]   }
2025-11-05 14:20:53 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-05 14:20:53 [kimi-k2-0905]   serve(attachFountain(myApp), { port: 8000 });
2025-11-05 14:20:53 [kimi-k2-0905]   console.log("[bridge] HTTP on :8000  |  Fountain TCP on :8081");
2025-11-05 14:20:53 [kimi-k2-0905] }
2025-11-05 14:20:53 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-05 14:20:53 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-05 14:20:53 [kimi-k2-0905] Drop-in checklist
2025-11-05 14:20:53 [kimi-k2-0905] 1. Save as `fountain-bridge.ts` (or paste contents into an existing file).
2025-11-05 14:20:53 [kimi-k2-0905] 2. Call `attachFountain(yourRouter)` once when you create your HTTP server.
2025-11-05 14:20:53 [kimi-k2-0905] 3. Fountain must still be running (`deno task slopfountain`) on TCP 8081.
2025-11-05 14:20:53 [kimi-k2-0905] 4. From anywhere in your codebase:
2025-11-05 14:20:53 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-05 14:20:53 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-05 14:20:53 [kimi-k2-0905] import { writeFountain } from "./fountain-bridge.ts";
2025-11-05 14:20:53 [kimi-k2-0905] writeFountain({ messages: [{ message: "ping", from: "webhook" }] });
2025-11-05 14:20:53 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-05 14:20:53 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-05 14:20:53 [kimi-k2-0905] That’s it—your legacy server now speaks Fountain without changing a single line of the original
2025-11-05 14:20:53 [kimi-k2-0905] sloppyhost logic.
2025-11-05 14:20:53 [kimi-k2-0905] 
