2025-11-05 14:30:14 [simon@midnightblue] is the socket code posix conformant?
2025-11-05 14:30:29 [kimi-k2-0905] Yes—everything in the header is POSIX.1-2001 (and later) conformant:
2025-11-05 14:30:29 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-05 14:30:29 [kimi-k2-0905] - `<sys/socket.h>`, `<arpa/inet.h>`, `<unistd.h>`
2025-11-05 14:30:29 [kimi-k2-0905] - `socket`, `connect`, `send`, `recv`, `close`, `shutdown`
2025-11-05 14:30:29 [kimi-k2-0905] - `sockaddrin`, `inetpton`, `htons`
2025-11-05 14:30:29 [kimi-k2-0905] - `MSG_NOSIGNAL` is POSIX.1-2008 (falls back to `EPIPE` on older kernels)
2025-11-05 14:30:29 [kimi-k2-0905] - Threading via `<pthread.h>` is POSIX; `std::thread` is implemented on top of it by every modern
2025-11-05 14:30:29 [kimi-k2-0905] libstdc++/libc++
2025-11-05 14:30:29 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-05 14:30:29 [kimi-k2-0905] The only Linux-specific detail is the literal `127.0.0.1` address string, which is still valid
2025-11-05 14:30:29 [kimi-k2-0905] POSIX—IPv4 dotted-decimal is required by IEEE Std 1003.1.
2025-11-05 14:30:29 [kimi-k2-0905] 
