2025-11-13 12:05:07 [simon@midnightblue] what do hoodoo gurus and violent femmes have in common?
2025-11-13 12:05:14 [kimi-k2-0905] Both bands broke out of ’80s indie scenes (Sydney and Milwaukee respectively) with stripped-down,
2025-11-13 12:05:14 [kimi-k2-0905] guitar-driven records on small labels, then found early U.S. college-radio love before mainstream
2025-11-13 12:05:14 [kimi-k2-0905] labels came calling.
2025-11-13 12:05:14 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-13 12:05:40 [simon@midnightblue] anything about the bass lines?
2025-11-13 12:05:49 [kimi-k2-0905] Both bands put the bass up front and gave it melodic, almost lead-guitar duties.
2025-11-13 12:05:49 [kimi-k2-0905] Rob Hirst (Hoodoo Gurus) and Brian Ritchie (Violent Femmes) play busy, hook-heavy lines that skip
2025-11-13 12:05:49 [kimi-k2-0905] the root-note clichés and carry whole riffs—think “(Let’s All) Turn On” or “Blister in the Sun.”
2025-11-13 12:05:49 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-13 12:06:17 [simon@midnightblue] other bands of the band with similar style / story
2025-11-13 12:06:25 [kimi-k2-0905] Here are a few that share the same “college-rock bass-out-front, indie-to-major” arc:
2025-11-13 12:06:25 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-13 12:06:25 [kimi-k2-0905] - The Replacements
2025-11-13 12:06:25 [kimi-k2-0905] - Soul Asylum
2025-11-13 12:06:25 [kimi-k2-0905] - The dB’s
2025-11-13 12:06:25 [kimi-k2-0905] - Guadalcanal Diary
2025-11-13 12:06:25 [kimi-k2-0905] - The Rainmakers
2025-11-13 12:06:25 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-13 12:06:48 [simon@midnightblue] how about morphine?
2025-11-13 12:06:58 [kimi-k2-0905] Morphine ticks the “bass-as-lead” box even harder—Mark Sandman’s two-string slide bass is
2025-11-13 12:06:58 [kimi-k2-0905] the entire guitar slot—but their story runs differently: formed in the late-’80s Boston
2025-11-13 12:06:58 [kimi-k2-0905] post-no-wave scene, stayed on indie label Rykodisc for most of their career, never really flirted
2025-11-13 12:06:58 [kimi-k2-0905] with the mainstream, and built a cult following on the back of a unique “low-rock” sound rather
2025-11-13 12:06:58 [kimi-k2-0905] than college-radio pop hooks.
2025-11-13 12:06:58 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-11-13 12:07:15 [simon@midnightblue] no wave?
2025-11-13 12:07:25 [kimi-k2-0905] “No wave” was a short-lived late-’70s New York reaction against punk’s tightening song forms:
2025-11-13 12:07:25 [kimi-k2-0905] abrasive, often horn-driven noise built on repetition and dissonance rather than verse/chorus
2025-11-13 12:07:25 [kimi-k2-0905] hooks. Morphine’s members came out of the Boston end of that mindset—minimal, dark, and
2025-11-13 12:07:25 [kimi-k2-0905] groove-centric—even though the band itself formed a decade later.
2025-11-13 12:07:25 [kimi-k2-0905] 
