2025-11-18 16:17:08 [simon@midnightblue] i want the first line to include platform i am on (macos)
2025-11-18 16:17:12 [gpt-5.1-2025] On macOS you can use the POSIX `uname` call to get the platform name at runtime and prepend it to
2025-11-18 16:17:12 [gpt-5.1-2025] your existing line.
2025-11-18 16:17:12 [gpt-5.1-2025] 
2025-11-18 16:17:12 [gpt-5.1-2025] Minimal example:
2025-11-18 16:17:12 [gpt-5.1-2025] 
2025-11-18 16:17:12 [gpt-5.1-2025] 
2025-11-18 16:17:12 [gpt-5.1-2025] #include <iostream>
2025-11-18 16:17:12 [gpt-5.1-2025] #include <sys/utsname.h>
2025-11-18 16:17:12 [gpt-5.1-2025] #include <v8.h>
2025-11-18 16:17:12 [gpt-5.1-2025] 
2025-11-18 16:17:12 [gpt-5.1-2025] std::string getPlatformName() {
2025-11-18 16:17:12 [gpt-5.1-2025] 	struct utsname buf{};
2025-11-18 16:17:12 [gpt-5.1-2025] 	if (uname(&buf) != 0) {
2025-11-18 16:17:12 [gpt-5.1-2025] 		return "unknown";
2025-11-18 16:17:12 [gpt-5.1-2025] 	}
2025-11-18 16:17:12 [gpt-5.1-2025] 	return std::string(buf.sysname) + " " + buf.release;
2025-11-18 16:17:12 [gpt-5.1-2025] }
2025-11-18 16:17:12 [gpt-5.1-2025] 
2025-11-18 16:17:12 [gpt-5.1-2025] int main() {
2025-11-18 16:17:12 [gpt-5.1-2025] 	std::cout << getPlatformName() << " - v8io - 0.0" << std::endl;
2025-11-18 16:17:12 [gpt-5.1-2025] 	std::cout << "V8::GetVersion(): " << v8::V8::GetVersion() << std::endl;
2025-11-18 16:17:12 [gpt-5.1-2025] 	return 0;
2025-11-18 16:17:12 [gpt-5.1-2025] }
