2025-11-18 16:24:33 [simon@midnightblue] i was thinking i would need a DSPPlatform next so i am not dependent
2025-11-18 16:24:43 [gpt-5.1-2025] A `DSPPlatform` layer is a good idea: keep your DSP/JS logic separate from “how do I talk to V8 (or
2025-11-18 16:24:43 [gpt-5.1-2025] something else) on this OS”.
2025-11-18 16:24:43 [gpt-5.1-2025] 
2025-11-18 16:24:43 [gpt-5.1-2025] You can think of it as:
2025-11-18 16:24:43 [gpt-5.1-2025] 
2025-11-18 16:24:43 [gpt-5.1-2025] - `DSPPlatform`: abstract interface your audio engine uses.
2025-11-18 16:24:43 [gpt-5.1-2025] - `V8DSPPlatform` (or `V8DSPTaskRunner`): one implementation using V8.
2025-11-18 16:24:43 [gpt-5.1-2025] - Later: `WasmDSPPlatform`, `NativeDSPPlatform`, etc.
2025-11-18 16:24:43 [gpt-5.1-2025] 
2025-11-18 16:24:43 [gpt-5.1-2025] At a high level:
2025-11-18 16:24:43 [gpt-5.1-2025] 
2025-11-18 16:24:43 [gpt-5.1-2025] 1. Define a small, engine‑agnostic interface, e.g.:
2025-11-18 16:24:43 [gpt-5.1-2025] 
2025-11-18 16:24:43 [gpt-5.1-2025] - load/prepare a DSP program
2025-11-18 16:24:43 [gpt-5.1-2025] - process samples or buffers
2025-11-18 16:24:43 [gpt-5.1-2025] - optional: reset, set parameters, etc.
2025-11-18 16:24:43 [gpt-5.1-2025] 
2025-11-18 16:24:43 [gpt-5.1-2025] 2. Implement that interface using V8, but keep all V8 types out of the interface itself.
2025-11-18 16:24:43 [gpt-5.1-2025] 
2025-11-18 16:24:43 [gpt-5.1-2025] Example of the shape (no code, just structure):
2025-11-18 16:24:43 [gpt-5.1-2025] 
2025-11-18 16:24:43 [gpt-5.1-2025] - `class DSPPlatform`
2025-11-18 16:24:43 [gpt-5.1-2025] 	- `bool loadProgram(const std::string &source)`
2025-11-18 16:24:43 [gpt-5.1-2025] 	- `bool processSample(double in, double &out)`
2025-11-18 16:24:43 [gpt-5.1-2025] 	- virtual destructor
2025-11-18 16:24:43 [gpt-5.1-2025] - `class V8DSPPlatform : public DSPPlatform`
2025-11-18 16:24:43 [gpt-5.1-2025] 	- Owns `v8::Isolate`, `v8::Context`, `v8::Global<v8::Function>` etc.
2025-11-18 16:24:43 [gpt-5.1-2025] 	- Implements `loadProgram` and `processSample` by calling JS.
2025-11-18 16:24:43 [gpt-5.1-2025] 
2025-11-18 16:24:43 [gpt-5.1-2025] Then your `main` (or audio engine) only sees `DSPPlatform*` and doesn’t care if underneath it’s V8
2025-11-18 16:24:43 [gpt-5.1-2025] 13.5, 14.0, or swapped to some other runtime.
2025-11-18 16:24:43 [gpt-5.1-2025] 
2025-11-18 16:24:43 [gpt-5.1-2025] If you’d like actual C++ definitions for `DSPPlatform` and a `V8DSPPlatform` skeleton, say so and
2025-11-18 16:24:43 [gpt-5.1-2025] I’ll write them out.
2025-11-18 16:24:43 [gpt-5.1-2025] 
2025-11-18 16:25:20 [simon@midnightblue] is v8::Platform abstract?
2025-11-18 16:25:25 [gpt-5.1-2025] Yes. `v8::Platform` is an abstract interface.
2025-11-18 16:25:25 [gpt-5.1-2025] 
2025-11-18 16:25:25 [gpt-5.1-2025] - It’s defined as a class with many pure virtual methods (e.g. task scheduling, time,
2025-11-18 16:25:25 [gpt-5.1-2025] foreground/background tasks, tracing hooks).
2025-11-18 16:25:25 [gpt-5.1-2025] - You normally don’t implement it yourself unless you’re deeply integrating V8 into a custom
2025-11-18 16:25:25 [gpt-5.1-2025] environment.
2025-11-18 16:25:25 [gpt-5.1-2025] 
2025-11-18 16:25:25 [gpt-5.1-2025] In most embedders you either:
2025-11-18 16:25:25 [gpt-5.1-2025] 
2025-11-18 16:25:25 [gpt-5.1-2025] 1. Use V8’s default implementation:
2025-11-18 16:25:25 [gpt-5.1-2025] 
2025-11-18 16:25:25 [gpt-5.1-2025] 
2025-11-18 16:25:25 [gpt-5.1-2025] std::unique_ptr<v8::Platform> platform = v8::platform::NewSingleThreadedPlatform();
2025-11-18 16:25:25 [gpt-5.1-2025] // or NewDefaultPlatform() / NewMultiThreadedPlatform() depending on version
2025-11-18 16:25:25 [gpt-5.1-2025] v8::V8::InitializePlatform(platform.get());
2025-11-18 16:25:25 [gpt-5.1-2025] v8::V8::Initialize();
2025-11-18 16:25:25 [gpt-5.1-2025] 
2025-11-18 16:25:25 [gpt-5.1-2025] 
2025-11-18 16:25:25 [gpt-5.1-2025] 2. Or provide your own subclass of `v8::Platform` if you need full control over task queues,
2025-11-18 16:25:25 [gpt-5.1-2025] timers, worker threads, etc.
2025-11-18 16:25:25 [gpt-5.1-2025] 
2025-11-18 16:25:25 [gpt-5.1-2025] This `v8::Platform` is independent of your own `DSPPlatform` idea; you can name your class
2025-11-18 16:25:25 [gpt-5.1-2025] `DSPPlatform` without any conflict.
2025-11-18 16:25:25 [gpt-5.1-2025] 
2025-11-18 16:26:14 [simon@midnightblue] does the code for the runner need a platform?
2025-11-18 16:26:20 [gpt-5.1-2025] Yes, somewhere in your process you must create and initialize a `v8::Platform` before using most of
2025-11-18 16:26:20 [gpt-5.1-2025] the V8 APIs (including creating an `Isolate` for your runner).
2025-11-18 16:26:20 [gpt-5.1-2025] 
2025-11-18 16:26:20 [gpt-5.1-2025] Key points:
2025-11-18 16:26:20 [gpt-5.1-2025] 
2025-11-18 16:26:20 [gpt-5.1-2025] - You need a `v8::Platform` once per process, not once per `DspTaskRunner`.
2025-11-18 16:26:20 [gpt-5.1-2025] - Typical pattern:
2025-11-18 16:26:20 [gpt-5.1-2025] 
2025-11-18 16:26:20 [gpt-5.1-2025] 
2025-11-18 16:26:20 [gpt-5.1-2025] 	int main() {
2025-11-18 16:26:20 [gpt-5.1-2025] 		// 1. Create a Platform (usually once, at startup)
2025-11-18 16:26:20 [gpt-5.1-2025] 		std::unique_ptr<v8::Platform> platform = v8::platform::NewSingleThreadedPlatform();
2025-11-18 16:26:20 [gpt-5.1-2025] 		v8::V8::InitializePlatform(platform.get());
2025-11-18 16:26:20 [gpt-5.1-2025] 		v8::V8::Initialize();
2025-11-18 16:26:20 [gpt-5.1-2025] 
2025-11-18 16:26:20 [gpt-5.1-2025] 		// 2. Now you can safely create DspTaskRunner, isolates, etc.
2025-11-18 16:26:20 [gpt-5.1-2025] 		DspTaskRunner runner;
2025-11-18 16:26:20 [gpt-5.1-2025] 		// use runner...
2025-11-18 16:26:20 [gpt-5.1-2025] 
2025-11-18 16:26:20 [gpt-5.1-2025] 		// 3. Shutdown at end (optional but good practice)
2025-11-18 16:26:20 [gpt-5.1-2025] 		v8::V8::Dispose();
2025-11-18 16:26:20 [gpt-5.1-2025] 		v8::V8::ShutdownPlatform();
2025-11-18 16:26:20 [gpt-5.1-2025] 		return 0;
2025-11-18 16:26:20 [gpt-5.1-2025] 	}
2025-11-18 16:26:20 [gpt-5.1-2025] 
2025-11-18 16:26:20 [gpt-5.1-2025] 
2025-11-18 16:26:20 [gpt-5.1-2025] - Your `DspTaskRunner` class itself doesn’t need to know about `v8::Platform` if you do this
2025-11-18 16:26:20 [gpt-5.1-2025] initialization in `main` (or some higher‑level bootstrap). It just assumes V8 is initialized.
2025-11-18 16:26:20 [gpt-5.1-2025] 
2025-11-18 16:26:20 [gpt-5.1-2025] If you want, I can show a minimal full file that adds the platform init around your existing runner
2025-11-18 16:26:20 [gpt-5.1-2025] skeleton.
2025-11-18 16:26:20 [gpt-5.1-2025] 
