2025-11-19 10:06:31 [simon@midnightblue] 	void* GetRandomMmapAddr() override {		std::cout << "GetRandomMmapAddr" << std::endl << std::flush;		return nullptr;	}
2025-11-19 10:06:41 [gpt-5.1-2025] `GetRandomMmapAddr()` must return an address that satisfies V8’s sandbox / pointer compression
2025-11-19 10:06:41 [gpt-5.1-2025] expectations. Returning `nullptr` will usually trip V8’s DCHECKs or cause allocation failures when
2025-11-19 10:06:41 [gpt-5.1-2025] `V8ENABLESANDBOX` / `V8COMPRESSPOINTERS` are on.
2025-11-19 10:06:41 [gpt-5.1-2025] 
2025-11-19 10:06:41 [gpt-5.1-2025] You have two practical options:
2025-11-19 10:06:41 [gpt-5.1-2025] 
2025-11-19 10:06:41 [gpt-5.1-2025] 1. Use the default V8 PageAllocator instead of your own (recommended for now).
2025-11-19 10:06:41 [gpt-5.1-2025] 2. Implement `GetRandomMmapAddr()` by delegating to a real random allocator.
2025-11-19 10:06:41 [gpt-5.1-2025] 
2025-11-19 10:06:41 [gpt-5.1-2025] 1. Use the default PageAllocator
2025-11-19 10:06:41 [gpt-5.1-2025] 
2025-11-19 10:06:41 [gpt-5.1-2025] If you don’t need custom `mmap` control, drop `NitroPageAllocator` completely and don’t override
2025-11-19 10:06:41 [gpt-5.1-2025] `GetPageAllocator()` in `NitroPlatformWithPool`. That lets V8 manage its own sandbox layout, which
2025-11-19 10:06:41 [gpt-5.1-2025] is the simplest way to get a working build.
2025-11-19 10:06:41 [gpt-5.1-2025] 
2025-11-19 10:06:41 [gpt-5.1-2025] In `NitroPlatformWithPool`:
2025-11-19 10:06:41 [gpt-5.1-2025] 
2025-11-19 10:06:41 [gpt-5.1-2025] 
2025-11-19 10:06:41 [gpt-5.1-2025] v8::PageAllocator* GetPageAllocator() override {
2025-11-19 10:06:41 [gpt-5.1-2025] 	return nullptr; // Use V8's internal default
2025-11-19 10:06:41 [gpt-5.1-2025] }
2025-11-19 10:06:41 [gpt-5.1-2025] 
2025-11-19 10:06:41 [gpt-5.1-2025] 
2025-11-19 10:06:41 [gpt-5.1-2025] And you can delete `NitroPageAllocator` for now.
2025-11-19 10:06:41 [gpt-5.1-2025] 
2025-11-19 10:06:41 [gpt-5.1-2025] 2. Properly implement `GetRandomMmapAddr`
2025-11-19 10:06:41 [gpt-5.1-2025] 
2025-11-19 10:06:41 [gpt-5.1-2025] If you really want a custom allocator, `GetRandomMmapAddr()` should return a random-ish
2025-11-19 10:06:41 [gpt-5.1-2025] hint address (not `nullptr`). A simple pattern is:
2025-11-19 10:06:41 [gpt-5.1-2025] 
2025-11-19 10:06:41 [gpt-5.1-2025] - `mmap` a small dummy region with `MAPPRIVATE | MAPANONYMOUS`.
2025-11-19 10:06:41 [gpt-5.1-2025] - Immediately `munmap` it.
2025-11-19 10:06:41 [gpt-5.1-2025] - Return that address as the hint.
2025-11-19 10:06:41 [gpt-5.1-2025] 
2025-11-19 10:06:41 [gpt-5.1-2025] On Linux that’s a common way to get an ASLR-based “random” address. You should also respect page
2025-11-19 10:06:41 [gpt-5.1-2025] alignment and maybe some sandbox constraints, but the above is the basic idea.
2025-11-19 10:06:41 [gpt-5.1-2025] 
2025-11-19 10:06:41 [gpt-5.1-2025] If you want, I can outline or provide a concrete implementation of `GetRandomMmapAddr()` for Linux
2025-11-19 10:06:41 [gpt-5.1-2025] specifically.
2025-11-19 10:06:41 [gpt-5.1-2025] 
2025-11-19 10:07:11 [gpt-5.1-2025] A few things worth flagging in the current header/source set.
2025-11-19 10:07:11 [gpt-5.1-2025] 
2025-11-19 10:07:11 [gpt-5.1-2025] 1. `GetRandomMmapAddr()` returning `nullptr`
2025-11-19 10:07:11 [gpt-5.1-2025] 
2025-11-19 10:07:11 [gpt-5.1-2025] With `V8ENABLESANDBOX` / `V8COMPRESSPOINTERS` enabled, V8 uses
2025-11-19 10:07:11 [gpt-5.1-2025] `GetRandomMmapAddr()` as a hint to place the sandbox / cage. Returning `nullptr` can be acceptable
2025-11-19 10:07:11 [gpt-5.1-2025] as “no hint” on some platforms, but in newer V8 it tends to be used more aggressively and can
2025-11-19 10:07:11 [gpt-5.1-2025] trigger DCHECKs / layout failures in debug builds.
2025-11-19 10:07:11 [gpt-5.1-2025] 
2025-11-19 10:07:11 [gpt-5.1-2025] If you start seeing early crashes/asserts around `GetRandomMmapAddr`, either:
2025-11-19 10:07:11 [gpt-5.1-2025] 
2025-11-19 10:07:11 [gpt-5.1-2025] - Temporarily return `nullptr` but disable `V8ENABLESANDBOX` and
2025-11-19 10:07:11 [gpt-5.1-2025] `V8COMPRESSPOINTERS`, or
2025-11-19 10:07:11 [gpt-5.1-2025] - Implement a real hint, e.g. on POSIX:
2025-11-19 10:07:11 [gpt-5.1-2025] 	- `mmap` a small region,
2025-11-19 10:07:11 [gpt-5.1-2025] 	- keep the base aligned as needed,
2025-11-19 10:07:11 [gpt-5.1-2025] 	- `munmap` it and return the address as the hint.
2025-11-19 10:07:11 [gpt-5.1-2025] 
2025-11-19 10:07:11 [gpt-5.1-2025] If you want a concrete implementation for Linux/macOS, say so and I’ll sketch one tailored to your
2025-11-19 10:07:11 [gpt-5.1-2025] build flags.
2025-11-19 10:07:11 [gpt-5.1-2025] 
2025-11-19 10:07:11 [gpt-5.1-2025] 2. `GetPageAllocator()` override
2025-11-19 10:07:11 [gpt-5.1-2025] 
2025-11-19 10:07:11 [gpt-5.1-2025] This part in `NitroPlatformWithPool`:
2025-11-19 10:07:11 [gpt-5.1-2025] 
2025-11-19 10:07:11 [gpt-5.1-2025] 
2025-11-19 10:07:11 [gpt-5.1-2025] v8::PageAllocator* GetPageAllocator() override {
2025-11-19 10:07:11 [gpt-5.1-2025] 	std::cout << "GetPageAllocator" << std::endl << std::flush;
2025-11-19 10:07:11 [gpt-5.1-2025] 	return page_allocator_.get();
2025-11-19 10:07:11 [gpt-5.1-2025] }
2025-11-19 10:07:11 [gpt-5.1-2025] 
2025-11-19 10:07:11 [gpt-5.1-2025] 
2025-11-19 10:07:11 [gpt-5.1-2025] is syntactically fixed now (there was a missing semicolon in the earlier snippet). Just be aware
2025-11-19 10:07:11 [gpt-5.1-2025] that by supplying a custom allocator, you are responsible for:
2025-11-19 10:07:11 [gpt-5.1-2025] 
2025-11-19 10:07:11 [gpt-5.1-2025] - Correct permissions mapping (`SetPermissions`).
2025-11-19 10:07:11 [gpt-5.1-2025] - Using `alignment` properly in `AllocatePages` if V8 asks for non‑default alignment (you currently
2025-11-19 10:07:11 [gpt-5.1-2025] ignore it).
2025-11-19 10:07:11 [gpt-5.1-2025] - Not fighting the OS over address selection when V8 passes a non‑null `address` (you currently
2025-11-19 10:07:11 [gpt-5.1-2025] blindly pass it to `mmap`, which is OK for now but can be tightened later).
2025-11-19 10:07:11 [gpt-5.1-2025] 
2025-11-19 10:07:11 [gpt-5.1-2025] If anything goes wrong in sandbox layout, your `PageAllocator` is a prime suspect.
2025-11-19 10:07:11 [gpt-5.1-2025] 
2025-11-19 10:07:11 [gpt-5.1-2025] 3. `NumberOfWorkerThreads()`
2025-11-19 10:07:11 [gpt-5.1-2025] 
2025-11-19 10:07:11 [gpt-5.1-2025] You return `0`:
2025-11-19 10:07:11 [gpt-5.1-2025] 
2025-11-19 10:07:11 [gpt-5.1-2025] 
2025-11-19 10:07:11 [gpt-5.1-2025] int NumberOfWorkerThreads() override { return 0; }
2025-11-19 10:07:11 [gpt-5.1-2025] 
2025-11-19 10:07:11 [gpt-5.1-2025] 
2025-11-19 10:07:11 [gpt-5.1-2025] Given that you actually create a pool with `threads` threads, it’s more consistent (and helps some
2025-11-19 10:07:11 [gpt-5.1-2025] V8 heuristics) to return that:
