2025-11-19 10:10:30 [gpt-5.1-2025] Below I’ll describe what to change and why; if you want full drop‑in code I can post it explicitly.
2025-11-19 10:10:30 [gpt-5.1-2025] 
2025-11-19 10:10:30 [gpt-5.1-2025] ━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━
2025-11-19 10:10:30 [gpt-5.1-2025] 
2025-11-19 10:10:30 [gpt-5.1-2025] 1. Map `Permission` → `prot` correctly
2025-11-19 10:10:30 [gpt-5.1-2025] 
2025-11-19 10:10:30 [gpt-5.1-2025] Right now you hard‑code RW and only partially obey `permissions` in `SetPermissions`. You should:
2025-11-19 10:10:30 [gpt-5.1-2025] 
2025-11-19 10:10:30 [gpt-5.1-2025] - Decode `permissions` consistently in both `AllocatePages` and `SetPermissions`.
2025-11-19 10:10:30 [gpt-5.1-2025] - Keep no‐access as `PROT_NONE`.
2025-11-19 10:10:30 [gpt-5.1-2025] 
2025-11-19 10:10:30 [gpt-5.1-2025] Implementation idea:
2025-11-19 10:10:30 [gpt-5.1-2025] 
2025-11-19 10:10:30 [gpt-5.1-2025] 
2025-11-19 10:10:30 [gpt-5.1-2025] int ToProt(Permission permissions) {
2025-11-19 10:10:30 [gpt-5.1-2025] 	int prot = 0;
2025-11-19 10:10:30 [gpt-5.1-2025] 	if (permissions == Permission::kNoAccess) {
2025-11-19 10:10:30 [gpt-5.1-2025] 		return PROT_NONE;
2025-11-19 10:10:30 [gpt-5.1-2025] 	}
2025-11-19 10:10:30 [gpt-5.1-2025] 	if ((permissions & Permission::kRead) == Permission::kRead) {
2025-11-19 10:10:30 [gpt-5.1-2025] 		prot |= PROT_READ;
2025-11-19 10:10:30 [gpt-5.1-2025] 	}
2025-11-19 10:10:30 [gpt-5.1-2025] 	if ((permissions & Permission::kWrite) == Permission::kWrite) {
2025-11-19 10:10:30 [gpt-5.1-2025] 		prot |= PROT_WRITE;
2025-11-19 10:10:30 [gpt-5.1-2025] 	}
2025-11-19 10:10:30 [gpt-5.1-2025] 	if ((permissions & Permission::kExecute) == Permission::kExecute) {
2025-11-19 10:10:30 [gpt-5.1-2025] 		prot |= PROT_EXEC;
2025-11-19 10:10:30 [gpt-5.1-2025] 	}
2025-11-19 10:10:30 [gpt-5.1-2025] 	return prot;
2025-11-19 10:10:30 [gpt-5.1-2025] }
2025-11-19 10:10:30 [gpt-5.1-2025] 
2025-11-19 10:10:30 [gpt-5.1-2025] 
2025-11-19 10:10:30 [gpt-5.1-2025] Then use that in both `AllocatePages` and `SetPermissions`.
2025-11-19 10:10:30 [gpt-5.1-2025] 
2025-11-19 10:10:30 [gpt-5.1-2025] ━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━
2025-11-19 10:10:30 [gpt-5.1-2025] 
2025-11-19 10:10:30 [gpt-5.1-2025] 2. `AllocatePages`: alignment and address hints
2025-11-19 10:10:30 [gpt-5.1-2025] 
2025-11-19 10:10:30 [gpt-5.1-2025] Your current implementation ignores `alignment` and always passes `address` straight through to
2025-11-19 10:10:30 [gpt-5.1-2025] `mmap`. Minimal but more correct:
2025-11-19 10:10:30 [gpt-5.1-2025] 
2025-11-19 10:10:30 [gpt-5.1-2025] - If `alignment <= AllocatePageSize()`, just use normal `mmap`.
2025-11-19 10:10:30 [gpt-5.1-2025] - If `alignment > page_size`, allocate a slightly larger region and then trim / adjust if you
2025-11-19 10:10:30 [gpt-5.1-2025] really need that alignment. Many V8 builds never request > page size, so you can start with
2025-11-19 10:10:30 [gpt-5.1-2025] the simple path and add the complex one only if asserts hit.
2025-11-19 10:10:30 [gpt-5.1-2025] 
2025-11-19 10:10:30 [gpt-5.1-2025] For now, a pragmatic approach:
2025-11-19 10:10:30 [gpt-5.1-2025] 
2025-11-19 10:10:30 [gpt-5.1-2025] 
