2025-11-19 10:10:30 [gpt-5.1-2025] 3. `GetRandomMmapAddr`: give V8 a real hint
2025-11-19 10:10:30 [gpt-5.1-2025] 
2025-11-19 10:10:30 [gpt-5.1-2025] On Linux/macOS you can implement this as:
2025-11-19 10:10:30 [gpt-5.1-2025] 
2025-11-19 10:10:30 [gpt-5.1-2025] - `mmap` a small region.
2025-11-19 10:10:30 [gpt-5.1-2025] - Immediately `munmap` it.
2025-11-19 10:10:30 [gpt-5.1-2025] - Return that address.
2025-11-19 10:10:30 [gpt-5.1-2025] 
2025-11-19 10:10:30 [gpt-5.1-2025] This leverages ASLR for randomness and is close to what V8’s default allocator does. Example:
2025-11-19 10:10:30 [gpt-5.1-2025] 
2025-11-19 10:10:30 [gpt-5.1-2025] 
2025-11-19 10:10:30 [gpt-5.1-2025] void* GetRandomMmapAddr() override {
2025-11-19 10:10:30 [gpt-5.1-2025] 	std::cout << "GetRandomMmapAddr" << std::endl << std::flush;
2025-11-19 10:10:30 [gpt-5.1-2025] 
2025-11-19 10:10:30 [gpt-5.1-2025] 	size_t size = AllocatePageSize();
2025-11-19 10:10:30 [gpt-5.1-2025] 	int prot = PROT_NONE;
2025-11-19 10:10:30 [gpt-5.1-2025] 	int flags = MAP_PRIVATE | MAP_ANONYMOUS;
2025-11-19 10:10:30 [gpt-5.1-2025] 
2025-11-19 10:10:30 [gpt-5.1-2025] 	void* addr = mmap(nullptr, size, prot, flags, -1, 0);
2025-11-19 10:10:30 [gpt-5.1-2025] 	if (addr == MAP_FAILED) {
2025-11-19 10:10:30 [gpt-5.1-2025] 		return nullptr; // fall back; V8 may still cope
2025-11-19 10:10:30 [gpt-5.1-2025] 	}
2025-11-19 10:10:30 [gpt-5.1-2025] 
2025-11-19 10:10:30 [gpt-5.1-2025] 	// Immediately release; we just wanted an address hint.
2025-11-19 10:10:30 [gpt-5.1-2025] 	munmap(addr, size);
2025-11-19 10:10:30 [gpt-5.1-2025] 	return addr;
2025-11-19 10:10:30 [gpt-5.1-2025] }
2025-11-19 10:10:30 [gpt-5.1-2025] 
2025-11-19 10:10:30 [gpt-5.1-2025] 
2025-11-19 10:10:30 [gpt-5.1-2025] V8 will then use this as a hint for placing its sandbox / cages. If this starts tripping sandbox
2025-11-19 10:10:30 [gpt-5.1-2025] layout assertions, we can refine by masking / aligning the address or constraining it to a certain
2025-11-19 10:10:30 [gpt-5.1-2025] range.
2025-11-19 10:10:30 [gpt-5.1-2025] 
2025-11-19 10:10:30 [gpt-5.1-2025] ━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━
2025-11-19 10:10:30 [gpt-5.1-2025] 
2025-11-19 10:10:30 [gpt-5.1-2025] 4. `SetRandomMmapSeed`
2025-11-19 10:10:30 [gpt-5.1-2025] 
2025-11-19 10:10:30 [gpt-5.1-2025] You can store it and mix it into `GetRandomMmapAddr` if you want deterministic runs; otherwise a
2025-11-19 10:10:30 [gpt-5.1-2025] no‑op is acceptable for a first pass. For example:
2025-11-19 10:10:30 [gpt-5.1-2025] 
2025-11-19 10:10:30 [gpt-5.1-2025] 
2025-11-19 10:10:30 [gpt-5.1-2025] void SetRandomMmapSeed(int64_t seed) override {
2025-11-19 10:10:30 [gpt-5.1-2025] 	std::cout << "SetRandomMmapSeed: " << seed << std::endl << std::flush;
2025-11-19 10:10:30 [gpt-5.1-2025] 	random_seed_ = static_cast<uint64_t>(seed);
2025-11-19 10:10:30 [gpt-5.1-2025] }
2025-11-19 10:10:30 [gpt-5.1-2025] 
2025-11-19 10:10:30 [gpt-5.1-2025] 
2025-11-19 10:10:30 [gpt-5.1-2025] and use `randomseed` to offset your hint or choose size; but you can leave that for later.
2025-11-19 10:10:30 [gpt-5.1-2025] 
