2025-11-19 10:10:30 [gpt-5.1-2025] 5. `ReleasePages` and `DecommitPages`
2025-11-19 10:10:30 [gpt-5.1-2025] 
2025-11-19 10:10:30 [gpt-5.1-2025] Your `ReleasePages`:
2025-11-19 10:10:30 [gpt-5.1-2025] 
2025-11-19 10:10:30 [gpt-5.1-2025] 
2025-11-19 10:10:30 [gpt-5.1-2025] bool ReleasePages(void* address, size_t size, size_t new_size) override {
2025-11-19 10:10:30 [gpt-5.1-2025] 	if (new_size < size) {
2025-11-19 10:10:30 [gpt-5.1-2025] 		return munmap(static_cast<char*>(address) + new_size, size - new_size) == 0;
2025-11-19 10:10:30 [gpt-5.1-2025] 	}
2025-11-19 10:10:30 [gpt-5.1-2025] 	return true;
2025-11-19 10:10:30 [gpt-5.1-2025] }
2025-11-19 10:10:30 [gpt-5.1-2025] 
2025-11-19 10:10:30 [gpt-5.1-2025] 
2025-11-19 10:10:30 [gpt-5.1-2025] This is aggressive but generally fine; you are physically unmapping the tail. Some implementations
2025-11-19 10:10:30 [gpt-5.1-2025] instead use `madvise` or `mprotect` to keep the mapping but tell the kernel it can reuse the
2025-11-19 10:10:30 [gpt-5.1-2025] physical pages. If you start seeing problems with address space fragmentation, this would be the
2025-11-19 10:10:30 [gpt-5.1-2025] knob to adjust.
2025-11-19 10:10:30 [gpt-5.1-2025] 
2025-11-19 10:10:30 [gpt-5.1-2025] `DecommitPages` via `PROT_NONE` is OK as a simple implementation.
