2025-11-19 19:07:13 [simon@midnightblue] this
2025-11-19 19:07:25 [gpt-5.1-2025] You’re very close to a minimal custom‑platform V8 embedder. A few things to be aware of in this
2025-11-19 19:07:25 [gpt-5.1-2025] setup:
2025-11-19 19:07:25 [gpt-5.1-2025] 
2025-11-19 19:07:25 [gpt-5.1-2025] 1. Platform implementation
2025-11-19 19:07:25 [gpt-5.1-2025] - `NitroPlatformWithPool` derives from `v8::Platform` and implements the newer `*Impl` methods,
2025-11-19 19:07:25 [gpt-5.1-2025] which is good for recent V8.
2025-11-19 19:07:25 [gpt-5.1-2025] - `NumberOfWorkerThreads()` currently returns `0` but you actually create a pool with
2025-11-19 19:07:25 [gpt-5.1-2025] `std::thread::hardware_concurrency()`; you probably want to return that count instead.
2025-11-19 19:07:25 [gpt-5.1-2025] - `GetForegroundTaskRunner(...)` returns `nullptr`; for very simple synchronous code this is
2025-11-19 19:07:25 [gpt-5.1-2025] usually fine, but as soon as V8 wants to schedule foreground work or microtasks it may assert or
2025-11-19 19:07:25 [gpt-5.1-2025] misbehave. If you hit odd issues, you’ll need at least a trivial foreground `TaskRunner` tied to
2025-11-19 19:07:25 [gpt-5.1-2025] the main thread.
2025-11-19 19:07:25 [gpt-5.1-2025] 
2025-11-19 19:07:25 [gpt-5.1-2025] 2. Page allocator
2025-11-19 19:07:25 [gpt-5.1-2025] - `GetPageAllocator()` returning a custom `NitroPageAllocator` is okay as long as permissions and
2025-11-19 19:07:25 [gpt-5.1-2025] alignment behave as V8 expects.
2025-11-19 19:07:25 [gpt-5.1-2025] - On non‑Apple you use `mmap` with `MAPJIT` unconditionally; that’s a Darwin flag and will fail
2025-11-19 19:07:25 [gpt-5.1-2025] on Linux. You probably want `#ifdef APPLE` around `MAPJIT`.
2025-11-19 19:07:25 [gpt-5.1-2025] - `SetPermissions` on `NitroPageAllocator` remaps with `mmap(..., MAPFIXED | MAPANONYMOUS,
2025-11-19 19:07:25 [gpt-5.1-2025] ...)` instead of `mprotect`; that discards content and isn’t how V8 normally does it. If you’re
2025-11-19 19:07:25 [gpt-5.1-2025] experimenting, fine; if you want correctness, use `mprotect` like you did in `NitroPageAllocator2`.
2025-11-19 19:07:25 [gpt-5.1-2025] - Two allocators (`NitroPageAllocator` and `NitroPageAllocator2`) aren’t both used; you can drop
2025-11-19 19:07:25 [gpt-5.1-2025] one once you decide which behaviour you want.
2025-11-19 19:07:25 [gpt-5.1-2025] 
2025-11-19 19:07:25 [gpt-5.1-2025] 3. Sandbox / build flags
2025-11-19 19:07:25 [gpt-5.1-2025] - In `CMakeLists.txt` you define:
2025-11-19 19:07:25 [gpt-5.1-2025] 	- `V8ENABLESANDBOX`
2025-11-19 19:07:25 [gpt-5.1-2025] 	- `V8COMPRESSPOINTERS`
2025-11-19 19:07:25 [gpt-5.1-2025] 	- `V831BITSMISON64BIT_ARCH`
2025-11-19 19:07:25 [gpt-5.1-2025]   These must match how your `libv8` was actually built. If the library wasn’t built with
2025-11-19 19:07:25 [gpt-5.1-2025] those flags, you’ll get subtle crashes. Normally you don’t define them yourself; you include V8
2025-11-19 19:07:25 [gpt-5.1-2025] headers from the correct SDK and use the flags that library was compiled with.
2025-11-19 19:07:25 [gpt-5.1-2025] - If you’re using the homebrew V8 on macOS, it likely isn’t built with sandbox enabled. I’d remove
2025-11-19 19:07:25 [gpt-5.1-2025] those `targetcompiledefinitions` unless you know the library matches.
2025-11-19 19:07:25 [gpt-5.1-2025] 
2025-11-19 19:07:25 [gpt-5.1-2025] 4. Initialization / shutdown order
2025-11-19 19:07:25 [gpt-5.1-2025] - `InitializePlatform`, then `Initialize`, then create `NitroTaskRunner`/`Isolate` → good.
2025-11-19 19:07:25 [gpt-5.1-2025] - You dispose the `Isolate` in `NitroTaskRunner` before `V8::Dispose()` in `main` → also okay.
2025-11-19 19:07:25 [gpt-5.1-2025] - You commented out `V8::ShutdownPlatform()` and `platform.reset()`. For a short‑lived process this
2025-11-19 19:07:25 [gpt-5.1-2025] is usually fine, but to avoid leaks in tools like ASan/Valgrind, you should:
2025-11-19 19:07:25 [gpt-5.1-2025] 	- Ensure all isolates are destroyed.
2025-11-19 19:07:25 [gpt-5.1-2025] 	- Call `v8::V8::Dispose();`
2025-11-19 19:07:25 [gpt-5.1-2025] 	- Then `v8::V8::ShutdownPlatform();`
2025-11-19 19:07:25 [gpt-5.1-2025] 	- Then let `platform` go out of scope or `platform.reset()`.
2025-11-19 19:07:25 [gpt-5.1-2025] 
2025-11-19 19:07:25 [gpt-5.1-2025] 5. Thread pool logic
2025-11-19 19:07:25 [gpt-5.1-2025] - The delayed‑task logic is basically sound, but the `constcast` on `delayed.top()` is a
2025-11-19 19:07:25 [gpt-5.1-2025] bit ugly; a pattern that pops the top into a local then moves is cleaner, but functionally what you
2025-11-19 19:07:25 [gpt-5.1-2025] have works.
2025-11-19 19:07:25 [gpt-5.1-2025] - When `stop_` is set and there are pending delayed tasks, threads wake up and exit; tasks are
2025-11-19 19:07:25 [gpt-5.1-2025] dropped. That’s okay for a simple embedder, just be aware no draining happens.
2025-11-19 19:07:25 [gpt-5.1-2025] 
2025-11-19 19:07:25 [gpt-5.1-2025] 6. Isolate / context usage
2025-11-19 19:07:25 [gpt-5.1-2025] - `NitroTaskRunner` creates an isolate with a default `ArrayBuffer` allocator and no custom
2025-11-19 19:07:25 [gpt-5.1-2025] `Isolate::CreateParams::page_allocator` or embedder data. That means your custom
2025-11-19 19:07:25 [gpt-5.1-2025] `NitroPageAllocator` only affects V8’s internal platform allocations, not the isolate heap pages.
2025-11-19 19:07:25 [gpt-5.1-2025] If your intention is to drive the actual heap allocation via your own `PageAllocator`, you need to
2025-11-19 19:07:25 [gpt-5.1-2025] plug it into `CreateParams` (or into `v8::V8::SetPageAllocator`, depending on V8 version).
2025-11-19 19:07:25 [gpt-5.1-2025] - For simple “run some JS” this is fine, but sandbox + custom allocator usually go together; if you
2025-11-19 19:07:25 [gpt-5.1-2025] care about the sandbox, you’ll need to mirror what Chromium/V8 do in their embedder code.
2025-11-19 19:07:25 [gpt-5.1-2025] 
