2025-11-30 13:21:56 [simon@midnightblue] the two serial could transfer in same direction at same time?
2025-11-30 13:22:36 [gemini-3-pro] Here is the breakdown for the CBM Node software development, timing analysis, and protocol
2025-11-30 13:22:36 [gemini-3-pro] sequence.
2025-11-30 13:22:36 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-11-30 13:22:36 [gemini-3-pro] 1. Clocking and Handshaking
2025-11-30 13:22:36 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-11-30 13:22:36 [gemini-3-pro] There are two distinct modes of transfer available on this hardware.
2025-11-30 13:22:36 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-11-30 13:22:36 [gemini-3-pro] • A. Parallel Mode (PB0-PB7)*
2025-11-30 13:22:36 [gemini-3-pro] There is no shared "clock" wire for the parallel bus. It requires Asynchronous Handshaking.
2025-11-30 13:22:36 [gemini-3-pro] • Pico to C64: Pico drives data to 74LVC245, asserts `/OE`, then pulses `FLAG2` (User Port
2025-11-30 13:22:36 [gemini-3-pro] pin B) low. C64 detects this, reads the byte.
2025-11-30 13:22:36 [gemini-3-pro] • C64 to Pico: C64 writes to Port B ($DD01). The CIA2 chip automatically pulses `PC2` (User
2025-11-30 13:22:36 [gemini-3-pro] Port pin 8) low for one cycle on write. The Pico must detect this `PC2` pulse to trigger the
2025-11-30 13:22:36 [gemini-3-pro] 74HC165 latch sequence.
2025-11-30 13:22:36 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-11-30 13:22:36 [gemini-3-pro] • B. Serial Mode (SP1/SP2)*
2025-11-30 13:22:36 [gemini-3-pro] • Clock: The `CNT1` and `CNT2` lines are bidirectional clocks.
2025-11-30 13:22:36 [gemini-3-pro] • Simultaneity: Yes. CIA1 (SP1) and CIA2 (SP2) are separate chips. They can transfer data
2025-11-30 13:22:36 [gemini-3-pro] in the same direction at the same time, or full-duplex (one in, one out).
2025-11-30 13:22:36 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-11-30 13:22:36 [gemini-3-pro] 2. 6510 Assembly Timing (Bulk Read)
2025-11-30 13:22:36 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-11-30 13:22:36 [gemini-3-pro] Your proposed `STA buffer, X` approach is valid.
2025-11-30 13:22:36 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-11-30 13:22:36 [gemini-3-pro] • `STA $abs, X`: 5 Cycles.
2025-11-30 13:22:36 [gemini-3-pro] • `BIT $abs`: 4 Cycles.
2025-11-30 13:22:36 [gemini-3-pro] • `BPL/BMI/BVC/BVS`: 2 Cycles (3 if branch taken).
2025-11-30 13:22:36 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-11-30 13:22:36 [gemini-3-pro] • Theoretical Polling Loop (C64 receiving):*
2025-11-30 13:22:36 [gemini-3-pro] To receive data fast, you poll the Interrupt Control Register (ICR) for the FLAG bit.
2025-11-30 13:22:36 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-11-30 13:22:36 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-11-30 13:22:36 [gemini-3-pro] ; C64 receiving bulk data from Pico
2025-11-30 13:22:36 [gemini-3-pro] ; X = index
2025-11-30 13:22:36 [gemini-3-pro] rx_loop:
2025-11-30 13:22:36 [gemini-3-pro] 	lda $dd0d   ; Read CIA2 ICR (clears flags)
2025-11-30 13:22:36 [gemini-3-pro] 	and #$10    ; Check FLAG bit (Bit 4 corresponds to FLAG pin)
2025-11-30 13:22:36 [gemini-3-pro] 	beq rx_loop ; If zero, no flag received yet (4 cycles)
2025-11-30 13:22:36 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-11-30 13:22:36 [gemini-3-pro] 	lda $dd01   ; Read data from Port B (4 cycles)
2025-11-30 13:22:36 [gemini-3-pro] 	sta $0400,x ; Store in screen memory/buffer (5 cycles)
2025-11-30 13:22:36 [gemini-3-pro] 	inx         ; Increment index (2 cycles)
2025-11-30 13:22:36 [gemini-3-pro] 	bne rx_loop ; Loop if not zero (3 cycles)
2025-11-30 13:22:36 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-11-30 13:22:36 [gemini-3-pro] • Total Loop: ~18-20 cycles per byte.
2025-11-30 13:22:36 [gemini-3-pro] • Speed: ~1MHz / 20 ≈ 50KB/sec (theoretical max for polled parallel).
2025-11-30 13:22:36 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-11-30 13:22:36 [gemini-3-pro] 3. Setup Guide & Protocol (CSV Sequence)
2025-11-30 13:22:36 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-11-30 13:22:36 [gemini-3-pro] Here is the logical flow for the Software Development Guide.
2025-11-30 13:22:36 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-11-30 13:22:36 [gemini-3-pro] Setup: 8-Bit Parallel Write (Pico → C64)
2025-11-30 13:22:36 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-11-30 13:22:36 [gemini-3-pro] • Prerequisites:*
2025-11-30 13:22:36 [gemini-3-pro] • Pico: Set GP2-GP9 (Data) as Output. Set GP16 (/OE) HIGH (Disable). Set GP15 (FLAG2) as
2025-11-30 13:22:36 [gemini-3-pro] Open Drain Output (Floating/High).
2025-11-30 13:22:36 [gemini-3-pro] • C64: Set CIA2 Port B (DD03) to Input (00). Enable FLAG interrupt or set up polling on
2025-11-30 13:22:36 [gemini-3-pro] $DD0D.
2025-11-30 13:22:36 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-11-30 13:22:36 [gemini-3-pro] • CSV Protocol Sequence:*
2025-11-30 13:22:36 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-11-30 13:22:36 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-11-30 13:22:36 [gemini-3-pro] Step, Actor, Action, Signal State, Description
2025-11-30 13:22:36 [gemini-3-pro] 1, Pico, Write Data, GP2-9 = Byte, Pico puts data on Transceiver inputs
2025-11-30 13:22:36 [gemini-3-pro] 2, Pico, Enable Output, GP16 = LOW, Transceiver drives C64 PB lines
2025-11-30 13:22:36 [gemini-3-pro] 3, Pico, Handshake Assert, GP15 = LOW, Pulls C64 FLAG2 line Low
2025-11-30 13:22:36 [gemini-3-pro] 4, C64, Detect, ICR Bit 4 = 1, C64 detects FLAG edge
2025-11-30 13:22:36 [gemini-3-pro] 5, C64, Read, LDA $DD01, C64 reads byte from bus
2025-11-30 13:22:36 [gemini-3-pro] 6, C64, Acknowledge*, PA2 = LOW, C64 manually toggles PA2 to tell Pico "Done"
2025-11-30 13:22:36 [gemini-3-pro] 7, Pico, Handshake Release, GP15 = Float, Pico releases FLAG2
2025-11-30 13:22:36 [gemini-3-pro] 8, Pico, Disable Output, GP16 = HIGH, Transceiver High-Z (Safe)
2025-11-30 13:22:36 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-11-30 13:22:36 [gemini-3-pro] • Note: Step 6 (Acknowledge) is optional if timing is fixed, but recommended for stability.*
2025-11-30 13:22:36 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-11-30 13:22:36 [gemini-3-pro] Setup: 8-Bit Parallel Read (C64 → Pico)
2025-11-30 13:22:36 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-11-30 13:22:36 [gemini-3-pro] • Prerequisites:*
2025-11-30 13:22:36 [gemini-3-pro] • Pico: GP16 (/OE) HIGH (Disable Transceiver). GP17/18/19/20 configured for SPI/Shift
2025-11-30 13:22:36 [gemini-3-pro] input.
2025-11-30 13:22:36 [gemini-3-pro] • C64: Set CIA2 Port B (DD03) to Output (FF).
2025-11-30 13:22:36 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-11-30 13:22:36 [gemini-3-pro] • CSV Protocol Sequence:*
2025-11-30 13:22:36 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-11-30 13:22:36 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-11-30 13:22:36 [gemini-3-pro] Step, Actor, Action, Signal State, Description
2025-11-30 13:22:36 [gemini-3-pro] 1, C64, Write Data, STA $DD01, C64 puts byte on PB lines
2025-11-30 13:22:36 [gemini-3-pro] 2, C64, Auto-Handshake, PC2 pulses Low, CIA2 hardware pulses PC2 automatically
2025-11-30 13:22:36 [gemini-3-pro] 3, Pico, Detect, GP20 (Shift2 Q7) or Interrupt, Pico detects PC2 edge (via spare pin or polling)
2025-11-30 13:22:36 [gemini-3-pro] 4, Pico, Latch, GP18 (SH/LD) Pulse, Capture state of PB lines into 74HC165
2025-11-30 13:22:36 [gemini-3-pro] 5, Pico, Shift, GP17 (CLK) Toggle, Pico reads in the byte via SPI
2025-11-30 13:22:36 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-11-30 13:22:36 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-11-30 13:22:36 [gemini-3-pro] 4. Developer Notes
2025-11-30 13:22:36 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-11-30 13:22:36 [gemini-3-pro] 1.  PC2 Handling: The 74HC165 `P6` pin on U2 is connected to `PC2`. To detect when the C64
2025-11-30 13:22:36 [gemini-3-pro] has written data, the Pico must read the shift register. Since the Pico is much faster, you can
2025-11-30 13:22:36 [gemini-3-pro] constantly shift in U2 to poll bit 6.
2025-11-30 13:22:36 [gemini-3-pro] 2.  Bit Command: Using `BIT` is excellent for checking the handshake line without modifying
2025-11-30 13:22:36 [gemini-3-pro] the Accumulator `A`, allowing you to keep data in `A` if needed, though usually, you consume the
2025-11-30 13:22:36 [gemini-3-pro] data immediately.
2025-11-30 13:22:36 [gemini-3-pro] 3.  Dual Serial: To use SP1 and SP2 simultaneously, you must handle `CNT1` and `CNT2`
2025-11-30 13:22:36 [gemini-3-pro] interrupts on the C64. This is CPU intensive. For bulk transfers, the Parallel Port (PB0-7) is
2025-11-30 13:22:36 [gemini-3-pro] preferred. Serial is better for streaming (like a modem).
2025-11-30 13:22:36 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-11-30 13:24:15 [simon@midnightblue] explain dual serial as separate concept for read and write
2025-11-30 13:24:57 [gemini-3-pro] Here is the breakdown for the CBM Node software development and timing logic.
2025-11-30 13:24:57 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-11-30 13:24:57 [gemini-3-pro] 1. C64 Addressing Logic
2025-11-30 13:24:57 [gemini-3-pro] 	a bulk transfer on node on cia means the c64 could decide where to write the bytes"
2025-11-30 13:24:57 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-11-30 13:24:57 [gemini-3-pro] • Correct.* The RP2040 places a byte on the PB0-PB7 bus and signals ready. The C64 CPU
2025-11-30 13:24:57 [gemini-3-pro] (6510) reads that byte from the CIA register (`$DD01`) and executes a store instruction (`STA`,
2025-11-30 13:24:57 [gemini-3-pro] `STX`, `STY`) to place it into RAM. The RP2040 has no control over the C64's memory map; it acts as
2025-11-30 13:24:57 [gemini-3-pro] a FIFO stream.
2025-11-30 13:24:57 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-11-30 13:24:57 [gemini-3-pro] 2. Clocking Data (Handshaking)
2025-11-30 13:24:57 [gemini-3-pro] The User Port parallel bus is asynchronous. You do not "clock" it like SPI; you "handshake" it.
2025-11-30 13:24:57 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-11-30 13:24:57 [gemini-3-pro] • RP2040 to C64 (Write):
2025-11-30 13:24:57 [gemini-3-pro]     1.  RP2040 puts data on `PB0-PB7` (via 74LVC245).
2025-11-30 13:24:57 [gemini-3-pro]     2.  RP2040 pulls `FLAG2` LOW (Data Ready).
2025-11-30 13:24:57 [gemini-3-pro]     3.  C64 detects `FLAG2` (via CIA interrupt or polling).
2025-11-30 13:24:57 [gemini-3-pro]     4.  C64 reads `$DD01`.
2025-11-30 13:24:57 [gemini-3-pro]     5.  C64 pulses `PA2` LOW (Acknowledge).
2025-11-30 13:24:57 [gemini-3-pro]     6.  RP2040 detects `PA2` Low, releases `FLAG2`, and prepares next byte.
2025-11-30 13:24:57 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-11-30 13:24:57 [gemini-3-pro] 3. Setup Steps (CSV Sequence)
2025-11-30 13:24:57 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-11-30 13:24:57 [gemini-3-pro] • Scenario: Bulk Read (RP2040 sending data to C64)*
2025-11-30 13:24:57 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-11-30 13:24:57 [gemini-3-pro] ╭────────┬──────────┬─────────────────────────────────────┬─────────────────╮
2025-11-30 13:24:57 [gemini-3-pro] │  Step  │  Actor   │  Action                             │  Signal State   │
2025-11-30 13:24:57 [gemini-3-pro] ├────────┼──────────┼─────────────────────────────────────┼─────────────────┤
2025-11-30 13:24:57 [gemini-3-pro] │  :---  │  :---    │  :---                               │  :---           │
2025-11-30 13:24:57 [gemini-3-pro] │  1     │  RP2040  │  Writes Byte to 74LVC245 inputs     │  Bus Stable     │
2025-11-30 13:24:57 [gemini-3-pro] │  2     │  RP2040  │  Asserts /OE on 74LVC245            │  PB0-7 Active   │
2025-11-30 13:24:57 [gemini-3-pro] │  3     │  RP2040  │  Pulls FLAG2 Low (2N7000)           │  FLAG2 = 0      │
2025-11-30 13:24:57 [gemini-3-pro] │  4     │  C64     │  Polls CIA ICR or waits for IRQ     │  Detects Flag   │
2025-11-30 13:24:57 [gemini-3-pro] │  5     │  C64     │  Reads $DD01 (CIA Port B)           │  Data Captured  │
2025-11-30 13:24:57 [gemini-3-pro] │  6     │  C64     │  Toggles PA2 Low then High          │  Handshake      │
2025-11-30 13:24:57 [gemini-3-pro] │  7     │  RP2040  │  Detects PA2 edge (via GP10/Shift)  │  Ack Received   │
2025-11-30 13:24:57 [gemini-3-pro] │  8     │  RP2040  │  Releases FLAG2                     │  FLAG2 = 1      │
2025-11-30 13:24:57 [gemini-3-pro] │  9     │  RP2040  │  Loads next byte, repeat Step 1     │                 │
2025-11-30 13:24:57 [gemini-3-pro] ╰────────┴──────────┴─────────────────────────────────────┴─────────────────╯
2025-11-30 13:24:57 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-11-30 13:24:57 [gemini-3-pro] 4. 6510 Assembly & Timing
2025-11-30 13:24:57 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-11-30 13:24:57 [gemini-3-pro] 	could bulk read writing bytes at a time to ,x index by 6510 with poll be ok?"
2025-11-30 13:24:57 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-11-30 13:24:57 [gemini-3-pro] Yes. This is the standard "High Speed" parallel transfer method.
2025-11-30 13:24:57 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-11-30 13:24:57 [gemini-3-pro] 	how slows is sta?"
2025-11-30 13:24:57 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-11-30 13:24:57 [gemini-3-pro] • `STA $xxxx` (Absolute): 4 cycles.
2025-11-30 13:24:57 [gemini-3-pro] • `STA $xxxx,X` (Absolute Indexed): 5 cycles.
2025-11-30 13:24:57 [gemini-3-pro] • `BIT $xxxx`: 4 cycles.
2025-11-30 13:24:57 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-11-30 13:24:57 [gemini-3-pro] • Example Tight Loop (Polling PA2 on Port A):*
2025-11-30 13:24:57 [gemini-3-pro] Assuming `PA2` is connected to a pin we can poll (often reflected in Port A or ICR).
2025-11-30 13:24:57 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-11-30 13:24:57 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-11-30 13:24:57 [gemini-3-pro] ; C64 Receiving Data
2025-11-30 13:24:57 [gemini-3-pro] ; $DD00 = CIA2 Port A (Serial/Vic Bank/PA2)
2025-11-30 13:24:57 [gemini-3-pro] ; $DD01 = CIA2 Port B (User Port Data)
2025-11-30 13:24:57 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-11-30 13:24:57 [gemini-3-pro] 		ldx #$00
2025-11-30 13:24:57 [gemini-3-pro] loop:
2025-11-30 13:24:57 [gemini-3-pro] 		bit $DD00      ; 4 cycles - Check PA2 state (reflected in bit 2 usually)
2025-11-30 13:24:57 [gemini-3-pro] 		bne loop       ; 3 cycles - Wait for RP2040 to signal Ready (Low)
2025-11-30 13:24:57 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-11-30 13:24:57 [gemini-3-pro] 		lda $DD01      ; 4 cycles - Read Data from RP2040
2025-11-30 13:24:57 [gemini-3-pro] 		sta $0400,x    ; 5 cycles - Store in Screen RAM
2025-11-30 13:24:57 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-11-30 13:24:57 [gemini-3-pro] 		; Pulse PC2/PA2 to Ack (Optional, depends on hardware wiring)
2025-11-30 13:24:57 [gemini-3-pro] 		; toggle code here (~4-6 cycles)
2025-11-30 13:24:57 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-11-30 13:24:57 [gemini-3-pro] 		inx            ; 2 cycles
2025-11-30 13:24:57 [gemini-3-pro] 		bne loop       ; 3 cycles
2025-11-30 13:24:57 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-11-30 13:24:57 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-11-30 13:24:57 [gemini-3-pro] • Throughput Calculation:*
2025-11-30 13:24:57 [gemini-3-pro] • Approx 20-25 cycles per byte in a tight poll loop.
2025-11-30 13:24:57 [gemini-3-pro] • C64 Clock: 1.02 MHz (PAL) / 0.985 MHz (NTSC).
2025-11-30 13:24:57 [gemini-3-pro] • Max theoretical speed: ~40KB/s to 50KB/s using pure software polling.
2025-11-30 13:24:57 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-11-30 13:24:57 [gemini-3-pro] 	bit command looks nearly correct?"
2025-11-30 13:24:57 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-11-30 13:24:57 [gemini-3-pro] Yes. `BIT` is excellent because it samples memory bits 7 (N flag) and 6 (V flag) directly into the
2025-11-30 13:24:57 [gemini-3-pro] status register without modifying the Accumulator. If you wire the Handshake line to CIA Port A Bit
2025-11-30 13:24:57 [gemini-3-pro] 6 or 7, you can branch immediately with `BVC` or `BPL`.
2025-11-30 13:24:57 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-11-30 13:24:57 [gemini-3-pro] 5. Dual Serial (SP1 / SP2)
2025-11-30 13:24:57 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-11-30 13:24:57 [gemini-3-pro] 	the two serial could transfer in same direction at same time?"
2025-11-30 13:24:57 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-11-30 13:24:57 [gemini-3-pro] • Yes.*
2025-11-30 13:24:57 [gemini-3-pro] The CIA shift registers (SP pin) are independent hardware blocks.
2025-11-30 13:24:57 [gemini-3-pro] • Hardware: You have `SP1` (CIA 1) and `SP2` (CIA 2) on the User Port edge connector.
2025-11-30 13:24:57 [gemini-3-pro] • Logic: If you set both CIAs to "Input Mode" and feed them separate clocks (`CNT1`,
2025-11-30 13:24:57 [gemini-3-pro] `CNT2`), the C64 receives 2 bits simultaneously (one in CIA1 SDR, one in CIA2 SDR).
2025-11-30 13:24:57 [gemini-3-pro] • Software: The CPU must read two registers (`DC0C` and `DD0C`) to gather the data.
2025-11-30 13:24:57 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-11-30 13:24:57 [gemini-3-pro] 	explain dual serial as separate concept for read and write"
2025-11-30 13:24:57 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-11-30 13:24:57 [gemini-3-pro] This is Full Duplex.
2025-11-30 13:24:57 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-11-30 13:24:57 [gemini-3-pro] 1.  Lane 1 (SP1 + CNT1): Configured as Output.
2025-11-30 13:24:57 [gemini-3-pro]     *   C64 writes to SDR. CNT1 generates clock. RP2040 reads bitstream.
2025-11-30 13:24:57 [gemini-3-pro]     *   Used for sending commands or upload requests to the RP2040.
2025-11-30 13:24:57 [gemini-3-pro] 2.  Lane 2 (SP2 + CNT2): Configured as Input.
2025-11-30 13:24:57 [gemini-3-pro]     *   RP2040 drives SP2 data and CNT2 clock.
2025-11-30 13:24:57 [gemini-3-pro]     *   C64 receives data in SDR.
2025-11-30 13:24:57 [gemini-3-pro]     *   Used for streaming data back to the C64.
2025-11-30 13:24:57 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-11-30 13:24:57 [gemini-3-pro] • Advantage:* No bus turnaround time. The C64 can send a "Stop" command on SP1 while
2025-11-30 13:24:57 [gemini-3-pro] simultaneously receiving video data on SP2.
2025-11-30 13:24:57 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-11-30 13:24:57 [gemini-3-pro] RP2040 Implementation Note
2025-11-30 13:24:57 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-11-30 13:24:57 [gemini-3-pro] Since the RP2040 is much faster (133MHz+) than the C64 (1MHz), you should use the RP2040 PIO to
2025-11-30 13:24:57 [gemini-3-pro] simulate the 74HC logic or handle the handshaking.
2025-11-30 13:24:57 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-11-30 13:24:57 [gemini-3-pro] • Pseudo-code for RP2040 Write Loop:*
2025-11-30 13:24:57 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-11-30 13:24:57 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-11-30 13:24:57 [gemini-3-pro] while (bytes_left > 0) {
2025-11-30 13:24:57 [gemini-3-pro] 	// 1. Put byte on GPIO (PB0-PB7)
2025-11-30 13:24:57 [gemini-3-pro] 	gpio_put_masked(0xFF << 2, current_byte << 2);
2025-11-30 13:24:57 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-11-30 13:24:57 [gemini-3-pro] 	// 2. Assert FLAG2 (Low)
2025-11-30 13:24:57 [gemini-3-pro] 	gpio_put(GP15_FLAG2, 0);
2025-11-30 13:24:57 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-11-30 13:24:57 [gemini-3-pro] 	// 3. Wait for C64 to Ack (PA2 Low)
2025-11-30 13:24:57 [gemini-3-pro] 	// PA2 is read via 74HC165 or direct GPIO input depending on config
2025-11-30 13:24:57 [gemini-3-pro] 	while (gpio_get(GP10_PA2) == 1);
2025-11-30 13:24:57 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-11-30 13:24:57 [gemini-3-pro] 	// 4. Release FLAG2 (High)
2025-11-30 13:24:57 [gemini-3-pro] 	gpio_put(GP15_FLAG2, 1);
2025-11-30 13:24:57 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-11-30 13:24:57 [gemini-3-pro] 	// 5. Wait for C64 to release Ack (Handshake complete)
2025-11-30 13:24:57 [gemini-3-pro] 	while (gpio_get(GP10_PA2) == 0);
2025-11-30 13:24:57 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-11-30 13:24:57 [gemini-3-pro] 	bytes_left--;
2025-11-30 13:24:57 [gemini-3-pro] }
2025-11-30 13:24:57 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-11-30 13:24:57 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-11-30 13:29:27 [simon@midnightblue] could you try some pio code for the write loop
2025-11-30 13:30:13 [gemini-3-pro] Here is a breakdown of the cbm node logic, software implementation steps, and the requested
2025-11-30 13:30:13 [gemini-3-pro] PIO code.
2025-11-30 13:30:13 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-11-30 13:30:13 [gemini-3-pro] Hardware & Netlist Review
2025-11-30 13:30:13 [gemini-3-pro] The cbm node design is robust.
2025-11-30 13:30:13 [gemini-3-pro] • 74HC165 (Input): Great for expanding inputs, but note that reading the C64's handshake
2025-11-30 13:30:13 [gemini-3-pro] signal (`PC2`) requires a full shift-register cycle on the RP2040, which adds latency compared to a
2025-11-30 13:30:13 [gemini-3-pro] direct GPIO.
2025-11-30 13:30:13 [gemini-3-pro] • 74LVC245 (Output): Correct choice for 3.3V logic driving a 5V bus.
2025-11-30 13:30:13 [gemini-3-pro] • 2N7000 (Open Drain): Essential for the CIA control lines (CNT/SP/FLAG) which often have
2025-11-30 13:30:13 [gemini-3-pro] internal pull-ups or require open-collector logic.
2025-11-30 13:30:13 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-11-30 13:30:13 [gemini-3-pro] ━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━
2025-11-30 13:30:13 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-11-30 13:30:13 [gemini-3-pro] 1. C64 Bulk Transfer Logic
2025-11-30 13:30:13 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-11-30 13:30:13 [gemini-3-pro] How to clock data on the User Port
2025-11-30 13:30:13 [gemini-3-pro] The User Port exposes CIA2. You have two distinct methods available with this hardware:
2025-11-30 13:30:13 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-11-30 13:30:13 [gemini-3-pro] 1.  Parallel (PB0-PB7): Fastest. 8 bits at a time.
2025-11-30 13:30:13 [gemini-3-pro]     *   Write (RP2040 → C64): RP2040 puts byte on `GP2-GP9`, pulses `FLAG2` (GP15). C64
2025-11-30 13:30:13 [gemini-3-pro] detects `FLAG` interrupt, reads `$DD01`.
2025-11-30 13:30:13 [gemini-3-pro]     *   Read (C64 → RP2040): C64 writes `$DD01`, pulses `PC2`. RP2040 detects pulse,
2025-11-30 13:30:13 [gemini-3-pro] latches `74HC165`, reads data.
2025-11-30 13:30:13 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-11-30 13:30:13 [gemini-3-pro] 2.  Serial (SP1/SP2): Hardware accelerated shifting.
2025-11-30 13:30:13 [gemini-3-pro]     *   Clocking: The `CNT` pin is the clock.
2025-11-30 13:30:13 [gemini-3-pro]     *   RP2040 as Master: RP2040 toggles `CNT` (GP13/14) and drives `SP` (GP11/12). C64 CIA
2025-11-30 13:30:13 [gemini-3-pro] shift register automatically shifts in bits.
2025-11-30 13:30:13 [gemini-3-pro]     *   C64 as Master: C64 Timer A drives `CNT`. RP2040 must sample `SP` on `CNT` edges.
2025-11-30 13:30:13 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-11-30 13:30:13 [gemini-3-pro] "How slow is STA?" & Assembly Throughput
2025-11-30 13:30:13 [gemini-3-pro] The `STA` instruction itself is fast (4 cycles), but the loop overhead defines the speed.
2025-11-30 13:30:13 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-11-30 13:30:13 [gemini-3-pro] • 6510 Assembly Loop (Bulk Read from Parallel Port):*
2025-11-30 13:30:13 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-11-30 13:30:13 [gemini-3-pro] ; Loop to read data from RP2040 (Parallel Port B)
2025-11-30 13:30:13 [gemini-3-pro] ; Assumes RP2040 puts data on bus when C64 is ready
2025-11-30 13:30:13 [gemini-3-pro] LOOP:
2025-11-30 13:30:13 [gemini-3-pro]     BIT $DD00       ; 4 cycles - Check handshake (PA2/FLAG)
2025-11-30 13:30:13 [gemini-3-pro]     BVC LOOP        ; 3 cycles - Wait for Ready signal
2025-11-30 13:30:13 [gemini-3-pro]     LDA $DD01       ; 4 cycles - Read Port B
2025-11-30 13:30:13 [gemini-3-pro]     STA $0400, X    ; 5 cycles - Store in screen memory
2025-11-30 13:30:13 [gemini-3-pro]     INX             ; 2 cycles
2025-11-30 13:30:13 [gemini-3-pro]     BNE LOOP        ; 3 cycles
2025-11-30 13:30:13 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-11-30 13:30:13 [gemini-3-pro] • Total: ~21 cycles per byte.
2025-11-30 13:30:13 [gemini-3-pro] • Speed: @ 0.985MHz ≈ 47 KB/s.
2025-11-30 13:30:13 [gemini-3-pro] • Optimization: Unrolling the loop (repeating LDA/STA) can get closer to 70-80 KB/s.
2025-11-30 13:30:13 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-11-30 13:30:13 [gemini-3-pro] • Is `STA ,X` ok?*
2025-11-30 13:30:13 [gemini-3-pro] Yes. Using indexed addressing (`STA buffer, X`) is the standard way to fill a buffer.
2025-11-30 13:30:13 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-11-30 13:30:13 [gemini-3-pro] ━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━
2025-11-30 13:30:13 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-11-30 13:30:13 [gemini-3-pro] 2. Dual Serial Concept
2025-11-30 13:30:13 [gemini-3-pro] You have access to SP1 (CIA1) and SP2 (CIA2) on the edge connector.
2025-11-30 13:30:13 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-11-30 13:30:13 [gemini-3-pro] • Direction: Yes, both can transfer in the same direction simultaneously.
2025-11-30 13:30:13 [gemini-3-pro] • Concept: Treat them as a 2-bit wide serial bus (Double Data Rate).
2025-11-30 13:30:13 [gemini-3-pro]     *   RP2040 shifts bit 0 on SP1, bit 1 on SP2.
2025-11-30 13:30:13 [gemini-3-pro]     *   C64 reads both SDR registers.
2025-11-30 13:30:13 [gemini-3-pro]     *   Benefit: Doubles the serial throughput without the complexity of the 8-bit parallel
2025-11-30 13:30:13 [gemini-3-pro] handshake.
2025-11-30 13:30:13 [gemini-3-pro] • Full Duplex: Alternatively, use SP1 for TX and SP2 for RX to allow simultaneous
2025-11-30 13:30:13 [gemini-3-pro] bidirectional communication.
2025-11-30 13:30:13 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-11-30 13:30:13 [gemini-3-pro] ━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━
2025-11-30 13:30:13 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-11-30 13:30:13 [gemini-3-pro] 3. Software Dev Guide: CSV Sequence
2025-11-30 13:30:13 [gemini-3-pro] For a developer writing a tool to talk to the device.
2025-11-30 13:30:13 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-11-30 13:30:13 [gemini-3-pro] • Scenario: Bulk Write (RP2040 sending file to C64)*
2025-11-30 13:30:13 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-11-30 13:30:13 [gemini-3-pro] ╭─────────┬─────────────┬──────────┬───────────┬────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────╮
2025-11-30 13:30:13 [gemini-3-pro] │  Step   │  Action     │  Actor   │  Signal   │  Details                                                                           │
2025-11-30 13:30:13 [gemini-3-pro] ├─────────┼─────────────┼──────────┼───────────┼────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
2025-11-30 13:30:13 [gemini-3-pro] │  :---   │  :---       │  :---    │  :---     │  :---                                                                              │
2025-11-30 13:30:13 [gemini-3-pro] │  **1**  │  **Setup**  │  C64     │  `DDRB`   │  Set `$DD03` to `$00` (Port B Input). Enable FLAG interrupt.                       │
2025-11-30 13:30:13 [gemini-3-pro] │  **2**  │  **Ready**  │  C64     │  `PA2`    │  Drive `PA2` Low to signal "I am listening".                                       │
2025-11-30 13:30:13 [gemini-3-pro] │  **3**  │  **Data**   │  RP2040  │  `PB0-7`  │  PIO puts Byte 0 onto 74LVC245 inputs.                                             │
2025-11-30 13:30:13 [gemini-3-pro] │  **4**  │  **Clock**  │  RP2040  │  `FLAG2`  │  Pulse `FLAG2` Low (GP15).                                                         │
2025-11-30 13:30:13 [gemini-3-pro] │  **5**  │  **Read**   │  C64     │  `IRQ`    │  CIA2 detects FLAG edge. ISR reads `$DD01`.                                        │
2025-11-30 13:30:13 [gemini-3-pro] │  **6**  │  **Ack**    │  C64     │  `PA2`    │  Pulse `PA2` High/Low to acknowledge byte received (optional if timing is fixed).  │
2025-11-30 13:30:13 [gemini-3-pro] │  **7**  │  **Loop**   │  Both    │  -        │  Repeat steps 3-6 until buffer full.                                               │
2025-11-30 13:30:13 [gemini-3-pro] ╰─────────┴─────────────┴──────────┴───────────┴────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────╯
2025-11-30 13:30:13 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-11-30 13:30:13 [gemini-3-pro] ━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━
2025-11-30 13:30:13 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-11-30 13:30:13 [gemini-3-pro] 4. RP2040 PIO Code: Parallel Write Loop
2025-11-30 13:30:13 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-11-30 13:30:13 [gemini-3-pro] This PIO program pushes data to the 74LVC245 (GP2-GP9) and strobes the FLAG2 (GP15) line.
2025-11-30 13:30:13 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-11-30 13:30:13 [gemini-3-pro] • Assumptions:*
2025-11-30 13:30:13 [gemini-3-pro] 1.  GP2-GP9 are mapped to the 74LVC245 inputs.
2025-11-30 13:30:13 [gemini-3-pro] 2.  GP15 is the FLAG2 strobe (Open Drain via 2N7000).
2025-11-30 13:30:13 [gemini-3-pro] 3.  GP16 is /OE for the transceiver (must be LOW to drive).
2025-11-30 13:30:13 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-11-30 13:30:13 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-11-30 13:30:13 [gemini-3-pro] // cbm_parallel_write.pio
2025-11-30 13:30:13 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-11-30 13:30:13 [gemini-3-pro] .program cbm_write
2025-11-30 13:30:13 [gemini-3-pro] .side_set 1 opt pindirs
2025-11-30 13:30:13 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-11-30 13:30:13 [gemini-3-pro] ; Pin Mapping:
2025-11-30 13:30:13 [gemini-3-pro] ; OUT pins: GP2 - GP9 (8 bits data)
2025-11-30 13:30:13 [gemini-3-pro] ; SET pin:  GP15 (FLAG2 Strobe)
2025-11-30 13:30:13 [gemini-3-pro] ; SIDESET:  GP16 (/OE Transceiver Enable)
2025-11-30 13:30:13 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-11-30 13:30:13 [gemini-3-pro] public entry_point:
2025-11-30 13:30:13 [gemini-3-pro]     pull block          ; 1. Pull 32-bit word from TX FIFO into OSR
2025-11-30 13:30:13 [gemini-3-pro]     out pins, 8  side 0 ; 2. Output 8 bits to GP2-9, Assert /OE (Low)
2025-11-30 13:30:13 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-11-30 13:30:13 [gemini-3-pro]     set pins, 1         ; 3. Release FLAG2 (High/Float via 2N7000)
2025-11-30 13:30:13 [gemini-3-pro]     nop [5]             ; 4. Hold data stable
2025-11-30 13:30:13 [gemini-3-pro]     set pins, 0         ; 5. Drive FLAG2 Low (Signal C64)
2025-11-30 13:30:13 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-11-30 13:30:13 [gemini-3-pro]     nop [5]             ; 6. Hold Strobe
2025-11-30 13:30:13 [gemini-3-pro]     set pins, 1         ; 7. Release FLAG2
2025-11-30 13:30:13 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-11-30 13:30:13 [gemini-3-pro]                         ; 8. Loop automatically returns to start
2025-11-30 13:30:13 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-11-30 13:30:13 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-11-30 13:30:13 [gemini-3-pro] • CMake configuration snippet:*
2025-11-30 13:30:13 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-11-30 13:30:13 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-11-30 13:30:13 [gemini-3-pro] pico_generate_pio_header(TARGET ${PROJECT_NAME}
2025-11-30 13:30:13 [gemini-3-pro]     PIO_FILE cbm_parallel_write.pio)
2025-11-30 13:30:13 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-11-30 13:30:13 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-11-30 13:30:13 [gemini-3-pro] • C SDK Setup:*
2025-11-30 13:30:13 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-11-30 13:30:13 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-11-30 13:30:13 [gemini-3-pro] // Configure PIO
2025-11-30 13:30:13 [gemini-3-pro] uint offset = pio_add_program(pio, &cbm_write_program);
2025-11-30 13:30:13 [gemini-3-pro] pio_sm_config c = cbm_write_program_get_default_config(offset);
2025-11-30 13:30:13 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-11-30 13:30:13 [gemini-3-pro] // Map OUT pins (GP2-GP9)
2025-11-30 13:30:13 [gemini-3-pro] sm_config_set_out_pins(&c, 2, 8);
2025-11-30 13:30:13 [gemini-3-pro] for(int i=2; i<=9; i++) pio_gpio_init(pio, i);
2025-11-30 13:30:13 [gemini-3-pro] pio_sm_set_consecutive_pindirs(pio, sm, 2, 8, true);
2025-11-30 13:30:13 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-11-30 13:30:13 [gemini-3-pro] // Map SET pin (GP15 - FLAG2)
2025-11-30 13:30:13 [gemini-3-pro] sm_config_set_set_pins(&c, 15, 1);
2025-11-30 13:30:13 [gemini-3-pro] pio_gpio_init(pio, 15);
2025-11-30 13:30:13 [gemini-3-pro] pio_sm_set_consecutive_pindirs(pio, sm, 15, 1, true);
2025-11-30 13:30:13 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-11-30 13:30:13 [gemini-3-pro] // Map SIDE SET pin (GP16 - /OE)
2025-11-30 13:30:13 [gemini-3-pro] sm_config_set_sideset_pins(&c, 16);
2025-11-30 13:30:13 [gemini-3-pro] pio_gpio_init(pio, 16);
2025-11-30 13:30:13 [gemini-3-pro] pio_sm_set_consecutive_pindirs(pio, sm, 16, 1, true);
2025-11-30 13:30:13 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-11-30 13:30:13 [gemini-3-pro] // Initialize State Machine
2025-11-30 13:30:13 [gemini-3-pro] pio_sm_init(pio, sm, offset, &c);
2025-11-30 13:30:13 [gemini-3-pro] pio_sm_set_enabled(pio, sm, true);
2025-11-30 13:30:13 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-11-30 13:30:13 [gemini-3-pro] // Send Data
2025-11-30 13:30:13 [gemini-3-pro] pio_sm_put_blocking(pio, sm, 0x41); // Send 'A'
