2025-11-30 13:39:17 [simon@midnightblue] what existing devices available for c64 user port?
2025-11-30 13:40:00 [gemini-3-pro] Here is the breakdown for the CBM Node (User Port) software development and hardware logic.
2025-11-30 13:40:00 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-11-30 13:40:00 [gemini-3-pro] 1. Clocking Data on the User Port (Handshaking)
2025-11-30 13:40:00 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-11-30 13:40:00 [gemini-3-pro] The C64 User Port (CIA2) uses a specific handshake protocol for the 8-bit parallel port
2025-11-30 13:40:00 [gemini-3-pro] (`PB0`-`PB7`).
2025-11-30 13:40:00 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-11-30 13:40:00 [gemini-3-pro] • PC2 (Output from C64): The CIA automatically pulses this line low for one cycle whenever
2025-11-30 13:40:00 [gemini-3-pro] the CPU reads or writes the Port B register. This acts as an ACK.
2025-11-30 13:40:00 [gemini-3-pro] • FLAG2 (Input to C64): The external device (RP2040) pulls this line low to tell the CIA
2025-11-30 13:40:00 [gemini-3-pro] data is ready. This can trigger an IRQ or set a bit in the CIA interrupt register.
2025-11-30 13:40:00 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-11-30 13:40:00 [gemini-3-pro] • The Sequence:*
2025-11-30 13:40:00 [gemini-3-pro] 1.  RP2040 places data on `PB0-PB7` (via 74LVC245).
2025-11-30 13:40:00 [gemini-3-pro] 2.  RP2040 pulses `FLAG2` low (via GP15/2N7000).
2025-11-30 13:40:00 [gemini-3-pro] 3.  C64 detects `FLAG2` (via IRQ or polling `$DD0D`).
2025-11-30 13:40:00 [gemini-3-pro] 4.  C64 reads data from `$DD01` (Port B).
2025-11-30 13:40:00 [gemini-3-pro] 5.  C64 (CIA hardware) automatically pulses `PC2` low.
2025-11-30 13:40:00 [gemini-3-pro] 6.  RP2040 detects `PC2` low (via Shift Register U2) to know the byte was received.
2025-11-30 13:40:00 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-11-30 13:40:00 [gemini-3-pro] 2. Software Guide & CSV Sequence
2025-11-30 13:40:00 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-11-30 13:40:00 [gemini-3-pro] • Setup:*
2025-11-30 13:40:00 [gemini-3-pro] 1.  C64: Set CIA2 Port B (`DD03`) to Input (`00`) or Output (`FF`). Enable/Disable
2025-11-30 13:40:00 [gemini-3-pro] interrupts in `DD0D`.
2025-11-30 13:40:00 [gemini-3-pro] 2.  RP2040: Configure SPI for Shift Registers (74HC165). Configure GPIO for `FLAG2`
2025-11-30 13:40:00 [gemini-3-pro] (Output) and Transceiver direction.
2025-11-30 13:40:00 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-11-30 13:40:00 [gemini-3-pro] • Bulk Write (RP2040 → C64) CSV Steps:*
2025-11-30 13:40:00 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-11-30 13:40:00 [gemini-3-pro] Step, Actor, Action, Signal State
2025-11-30 13:40:00 [gemini-3-pro] 1, RP2040, Load 74LVC245, PB0-7 = Data Byte
2025-11-30 13:40:00 [gemini-3-pro] 2, RP2040, Assert Data, /OE = LOW
2025-11-30 13:40:00 [gemini-3-pro] 3, RP2040, Signal Ready, FLAG2 = LOW
2025-11-30 13:40:00 [gemini-3-pro] 4, C64, Poll/IRQ, Detect FLAG2
2025-11-30 13:40:00 [gemini-3-pro] 5, C64, Read Port, LDA $DD01
2025-11-30 13:40:00 [gemini-3-pro] 6, C64, Auto-Ack, PC2 Pulses LOW
2025-11-30 13:40:00 [gemini-3-pro] 7, RP2040, Read Shift Reg, Detect PC2 LOW
2025-11-30 13:40:00 [gemini-3-pro] 8, RP2040, Reset, FLAG2 = HIGH
2025-11-30 13:40:00 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-11-30 13:40:00 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-11-30 13:40:00 [gemini-3-pro] 3. C64 Assembly Speed (`STA`)
2025-11-30 13:40:00 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-11-30 13:40:00 [gemini-3-pro] Is `STA ,X` fast enough?
2025-11-30 13:40:00 [gemini-3-pro] • `STA $DD01` (Absolute) = 4 cycles.
2025-11-30 13:40:00 [gemini-3-pro] • `STA $DD01,X` (Absolute Indexed) = 5 cycles.
2025-11-30 13:40:00 [gemini-3-pro] • Loop Overhead: `INX` (2) + `CPX` (2) + `BNE` (3) = 7 cycles.
2025-11-30 13:40:00 [gemini-3-pro] • Total: ~12 cycles per byte best case (unrolled).
2025-11-30 13:40:00 [gemini-3-pro] • Polling Overhead: If polling `$DD0D` for the handshake, add ~6-10 cycles.
2025-11-30 13:40:00 [gemini-3-pro] • Throughput: At 1MHz, ~20 cycles/byte = 50kB/s. This is very fast for a C64.
2025-11-30 13:40:00 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-11-30 13:40:00 [gemini-3-pro] 4. Dual Serial Concept
2025-11-30 13:40:00 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-11-30 13:40:00 [gemini-3-pro] The C64 User Port exposes two separate shift registers from the CIAs:
2025-11-30 13:40:00 [gemini-3-pro] 1.  SP1 (CIA1): Pins 5 & 7 (Netlist maps SP1/SP2 to these).
2025-11-30 13:40:00 [gemini-3-pro] 2.  SP2 (CIA2): Pins 5 & 7 (Mapped differently in different docs, usually User Port has SP1
2025-11-30 13:40:00 [gemini-3-pro] and SP2).
2025-11-30 13:40:00 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-11-30 13:40:00 [gemini-3-pro] • Concept: Unlike the parallel port (8 bits at once), these are serial shift registers.
2025-11-30 13:40:00 [gemini-3-pro] • Simultaneity: Yes. They are on separate chips (CIA1 at `DCxx`, CIA2 at `DDxx`).
2025-11-30 13:40:00 [gemini-3-pro] • Direction: You can set SP1 to Receive (C64 <- RP2040) and SP2 to Transmit (C64 -> RP2040)
2025-11-30 13:40:00 [gemini-3-pro] simultaneously.
2025-11-30 13:40:00 [gemini-3-pro] • Benefit: Enables Full Duplex communication without complex timing on the parallel
2025-11-30 13:40:00 [gemini-3-pro] bus.
2025-11-30 13:40:00 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-11-30 13:40:00 [gemini-3-pro] 5. PIO Code (Write Loop)
2025-11-30 13:40:00 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-11-30 13:40:00 [gemini-3-pro] • Constraint: In your Netlist, the C64 inputs (`PC2`, `PB0-7` when reading) connect to
2025-11-30 13:40:00 [gemini-3-pro] 74HC165 Shift Registers*, not direct GPIOs.
2025-11-30 13:40:00 [gemini-3-pro] • The PIO cannot "wait" on `PC2` directly because it must clock the Shift Register to see the pin
2025-11-30 13:40:00 [gemini-3-pro] state.
2025-11-30 13:40:00 [gemini-3-pro] • Therefore, the PIO must drive the Shift Registers to perform the handshake.
2025-11-30 13:40:00 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-11-30 13:40:00 [gemini-3-pro] Here is a PIO program that pushes data to the 74LVC245 (Parallel) and handles the `FLAG2` strobe.
2025-11-30 13:40:00 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-11-30 13:40:00 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-11-30 13:40:00 [gemini-3-pro] // JS format for PIO assembly
2025-11-30 13:40:00 [gemini-3-pro] const pio_cbm_write = `
2025-11-30 13:40:00 [gemini-3-pro] .program cbm_write
2025-11-30 13:40:00 [gemini-3-pro] .side_set 1 opt ; Use side-set for FLAG2 (GP15)
2025-11-30 13:40:00 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-11-30 13:40:00 [gemini-3-pro] ; Pin mappings:
2025-11-30 13:40:00 [gemini-3-pro] ; OUT pins: GP2-GP9 (PB0-PB7)
2025-11-30 13:40:00 [gemini-3-pro] ; SET pin:  GP16 (/OE for 74LVC245)
2025-11-30 13:40:00 [gemini-3-pro] ; SIDE pin: GP15 (FLAG2)
2025-11-30 13:40:00 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-11-30 13:40:00 [gemini-3-pro] public entry_point:
2025-11-30 13:40:00 [gemini-3-pro]     pull block          ; 1. Pull 32-bit word (contains 8-bit data) from FIFO
2025-11-30 13:40:00 [gemini-3-pro]     out pins, 8         ; 2. Output lowest 8 bits to PB0-PB7
2025-11-30 13:40:00 [gemini-3-pro]     set pins, 0         ; 3. Assert /OE (Low) to drive bus
2025-11-30 13:40:00 [gemini-3-pro]     nop         side 0  ; 4. Assert FLAG2 (Low) to signal C64
2025-11-30 13:40:00 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-11-30 13:40:00 [gemini-3-pro]     ; WAIT FOR ACK (PC2)
2025-11-30 13:40:00 [gemini-3-pro]     ; NOTE: Since PC2 is on a shift register, PIO cannot wait on it directly.
2025-11-30 13:40:00 [gemini-3-pro]     ; The main CPU must read the shift register to confirm receipt,
2025-11-30 13:40:00 [gemini-3-pro]     ; then push the next byte to PIO.
2025-11-30 13:40:00 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-11-30 13:40:00 [gemini-3-pro]     nop         side 1  ; 5. Release FLAG2 (High)
2025-11-30 13:40:00 [gemini-3-pro]     set pins, 1         ; 6. Release /OE (High) - float bus
2025-11-30 13:40:00 [gemini-3-pro] `;
2025-11-30 13:40:00 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-11-30 13:40:00 [gemini-3-pro] // Helper to configure the state machine
2025-11-30 13:40:00 [gemini-3-pro] function cbm_write_init(pio, sm, offset, pin_base, pin_oe, pin_flag) {
2025-11-30 13:40:00 [gemini-3-pro]     // ... standard pio setup ...
2025-11-30 13:40:00 [gemini-3-pro]     // Map OUT to pin_base (8 pins)
2025-11-30 13:40:00 [gemini-3-pro]     // Map SET to pin_oe
2025-11-30 13:40:00 [gemini-3-pro]     // Map SIDESET to pin_flag
2025-11-30 13:40:00 [gemini-3-pro] }
2025-11-30 13:40:00 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-11-30 13:40:00 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-11-30 13:40:00 [gemini-3-pro] 6. PC Signal & Existing Devices
2025-11-30 13:40:00 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-11-30 13:40:00 [gemini-3-pro] • PC Signal: Yes, the Netlist connects `PC2` (Pin 8 on User Port) to `U2.5` (74HC165
2025-11-30 13:40:00 [gemini-3-pro] Input). You must shift `U2` to read the acknowledgment.
2025-11-30 13:40:00 [gemini-3-pro] • Existing Devices:
2025-11-30 13:40:00 [gemini-3-pro]     *   WiModem: Uses User Port for Wifi (Hayes command set).
2025-11-30 13:40:00 [gemini-3-pro]     *   GGLABS USER-232: RS232 interface.
2025-11-30 13:40:00 [gemini-3-pro]     *   Protovision 4-Player Interface: Uses the parallel port for joystick inputs.
2025-11-30 13:40:00 [gemini-3-pro]     *   Hitmen GeoCable: Parallel cable for transferring data between C64s.
2025-11-30 13:40:00 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-11-30 13:40:31 [simon@midnightblue] what existing devices available for c64 user port?
2025-11-30 13:40:46 [gemini-3-pro] The Commodore 64 User Port is a versatile I/O connector that exposes the parallel lines from the
2025-11-30 13:40:46 [gemini-3-pro] CIA chip and supports RS-232 serial communication. Devices available for this port generally fall
2025-11-30 13:40:46 [gemini-3-pro] into vintage and modern categories.
2025-11-30 13:40:46 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-11-30 13:40:46 [gemini-3-pro] • Communication*
2025-11-30 13:40:46 [gemini-3-pro] • Wi-Fi Modems: Modern ESP8266 or ESP32-based devices (e.g., WiModem232) that allow the C64
2025-11-30 13:40:46 [gemini-3-pro] to connect to BBSs over the internet via Telnet.
2025-11-30 13:40:46 [gemini-3-pro] • RS-232 Interfaces: Level shifters (like the VIC-1011 or modern equivalents) allowing
2025-11-30 13:40:46 [gemini-3-pro] connection to standard serial devices.
2025-11-30 13:40:46 [gemini-3-pro] • Vintage Modems: The Commodore 1650, 1660, and 1670 modems.
2025-11-30 13:40:46 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-11-30 13:40:46 [gemini-3-pro] • Input and Control*
2025-11-30 13:40:46 [gemini-3-pro] • 4-Player Joystick Adapters: Devices such as the Protovision or CGA adapters that allow
2025-11-30 13:40:46 [gemini-3-pro] two additional joysticks to be connected for multiplayer gaming.
2025-11-30 13:40:46 [gemini-3-pro] • Mouse Adapters: Certain specialized mouse interfaces utilize the User Port rather than
2025-11-30 13:40:46 [gemini-3-pro] the standard joystick ports.
2025-11-30 13:40:46 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-11-30 13:40:46 [gemini-3-pro] • Audio and MIDI*
2025-11-30 13:40:46 [gemini-3-pro] • Sound Samplers: Hardware for digitizing audio (e.g., Datel, Technosound).
2025-11-30 13:40:46 [gemini-3-pro] • MIDI Interfaces: Interface cards (e.g., Passport, Sequential Circuits) allowing the C64
2025-11-30 13:40:46 [gemini-3-pro] to control synthesizers.
2025-11-30 13:40:46 [gemini-3-pro] • DACs: Digital-to-Analog converters (similar to the Covox Speech Thing) for high-quality
2025-11-30 13:40:46 [gemini-3-pro] audio output.
2025-11-30 13:40:46 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-11-30 13:40:46 [gemini-3-pro] • Printing and Utility*
2025-11-30 13:40:46 [gemini-3-pro] • Centronics Parallel Interfaces: Adapters (e.g., Cardco, Xetec) allowing the use of
2025-11-30 13:40:46 [gemini-3-pro] standard parallel printers.
2025-11-30 13:40:46 [gemini-3-pro] • EPROM Programmers: Hardware for burning code onto ROM chips (e.g., The Promenade).
2025-11-30 13:40:46 [gemini-3-pro] • Diagnostic Harnesses: Used in conjunction with a diagnostic cartridge to test the
2025-11-30 13:40:46 [gemini-3-pro] system's I/O capabilities.
2025-11-30 13:40:46 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-11-30 13:43:06 [simon@midnightblue] does 1650 have progamming guide
2025-11-30 13:43:07 [simon@midnightblue] ?
2025-11-30 13:43:27 [gemini-3-pro] Here is an overview of devices for the Commodore 64 User Port and information regarding the 1650
2025-11-30 13:43:27 [gemini-3-pro] modem.
2025-11-30 13:43:27 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-11-30 13:43:27 [gemini-3-pro] Common User Port Devices
2025-11-30 13:43:27 [gemini-3-pro] The User Port (Port 2) exposes the CIA #2 chip, allowing for parallel and serial communications.
2025-11-30 13:43:27 [gemini-3-pro] Common devices include:
2025-11-30 13:43:27 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-11-30 13:43:27 [gemini-3-pro] • Modems: Original hardware (1650, 1660, 1670) and modern Wi-Fi modem emulators.
2025-11-30 13:43:27 [gemini-3-pro] • RS-232 Interfaces: TTL-to-RS232 voltage converters for connecting standard serial
2025-11-30 13:43:27 [gemini-3-pro] devices.
2025-11-30 13:43:27 [gemini-3-pro] • Printer Adapters: Centronics parallel interfaces (e.g., Cardco, Xetec) to use standard
2025-11-30 13:43:27 [gemini-3-pro] non-Commodore printers.
2025-11-30 13:43:27 [gemini-3-pro] • Audio Devices: Sound samplers and digitizers (e.g., Covox Voice Master).
2025-11-30 13:43:27 [gemini-3-pro] • Input Adapters: 4-player joystick adapters (e.g., CGA/Protovision style).
2025-11-30 13:43:27 [gemini-3-pro] • Utility Hardware: EPROM programmers and relay control boards for home automation.
2025-11-30 13:43:27 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-11-30 13:43:27 [gemini-3-pro] Commodore 1650 Programming Guide
2025-11-30 13:43:27 [gemini-3-pro] Yes, programming information for the Commodore 1650 "Automodem" is available, though it is not
2025-11-30 13:43:27 [gemini-3-pro] typically found in a standalone developer's SDK.
2025-11-30 13:43:27 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-11-30 13:43:27 [gemini-3-pro] The 1650 is a "pulse" dial modem that does not use the Hayes "AT" command set found in later
2025-11-30 13:43:27 [gemini-3-pro] modems. Instead, it is controlled directly via the C64 KERNAL RS-232 routines.
2025-11-30 13:43:27 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-11-30 13:43:27 [gemini-3-pro] • Where to find the documentation:*
2025-11-30 13:43:27 [gemini-3-pro] 1.  Commodore 1650 User Manual: Contains the specific BASIC examples for setting baud rates
2025-11-30 13:43:27 [gemini-3-pro] and initiating calls.
2025-11-30 13:43:27 [gemini-3-pro] 2.  Commodore 64 Programmer's Reference Guide (Chapter 6): Provides the technical details
2025-11-30 13:43:27 [gemini-3-pro] on the User Port pinouts and the RS-232 KERNAL parameters needed to control the device.
2025-11-30 13:43:27 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-11-30 13:43:27 [gemini-3-pro] If you require a BASIC example of how to open the channel and send data to the 1650, please request
2025-11-30 13:43:27 [gemini-3-pro] it.
2025-11-30 13:43:27 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-11-30 13:44:23 [simon@midnightblue] how about the 1670
2025-11-30 13:44:42 [gemini-3-pro] Here is an overview of devices for the User Port and programming details for the 1650 and 1670
2025-11-30 13:44:42 [gemini-3-pro] modems.
2025-11-30 13:44:42 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-11-30 13:44:42 [gemini-3-pro] Common C64 User Port Devices
2025-11-30 13:44:42 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-11-30 13:44:42 [gemini-3-pro] The User Port (PB0-PB7) allows for parallel and serial communications. Common devices include:
2025-11-30 13:44:42 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-11-30 13:44:42 [gemini-3-pro] • Modems: Commodore 1650, 1660, 1670, and various third-party Hayes-compatible modems
2025-11-30 13:44:42 [gemini-3-pro] (often requiring a voltage level converter).
2025-11-30 13:44:42 [gemini-3-pro] • RS-232 Interfaces: The VIC-1011A and third-party adapters used to convert the C64's TTL
2025-11-30 13:44:42 [gemini-3-pro] levels to standard RS-232 voltages for connecting PC serial peripherals.
2025-11-30 13:44:42 [gemini-3-pro] • Parallel Printer Interfaces: Cables (such as those by Cardco) that allowed the C64 to use
2025-11-30 13:44:42 [gemini-3-pro] standard Centronics parallel printers.
2025-11-30 13:44:42 [gemini-3-pro] • Sound Digitizers: Devices like the Covox Voice Master used the port for audio sampling.
2025-11-30 13:44:42 [gemini-3-pro] • EPROM Programmers: Hardware used to burn code onto ROM chips.
2025-11-30 13:44:42 [gemini-3-pro] • Multi-Player Adapters: Devices like the Protovision 4-player interface allow for two
2025-11-30 13:44:42 [gemini-3-pro] additional joysticks.
2025-11-30 13:44:42 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-11-30 13:44:42 [gemini-3-pro] Commodore 1650 (Automodem) Programming
2025-11-30 13:44:42 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-11-30 13:44:42 [gemini-3-pro] The Commodore 1650 is a 300-baud, pulse-dial modem. It does not contain an internal microcontroller
2025-11-30 13:44:42 [gemini-3-pro] or command interpreter.
2025-11-30 13:44:42 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-11-30 13:44:42 [gemini-3-pro] • Programming Guide: There is no distinct "programming guide" because the modem does not
2025-11-30 13:44:42 [gemini-3-pro] accept commands (like AT commands).
2025-11-30 13:44:42 [gemini-3-pro] • Control Method: It is controlled entirely via the C64 KERNAL's RS-232 routines. You
2025-11-30 13:44:42 [gemini-3-pro] program it using standard BASIC commands (`OPEN`, `CMD`, `PRINT#`, `GET#`, `CLOSE`) to manipulate
2025-11-30 13:44:42 [gemini-3-pro] the logical file associated with device 2 (the RS-232 port).
2025-11-30 13:44:42 [gemini-3-pro] • Documentation: The standard user manual provides the necessary BASIC examples to open the
2025-11-30 13:44:42 [gemini-3-pro] channel and send data.
2025-11-30 13:44:42 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-11-30 13:44:42 [gemini-3-pro] Commodore 1670 Programming
2025-11-30 13:44:42 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-11-30 13:44:42 [gemini-3-pro] The Commodore 1670 is a 1200-baud "smart" modem. Unlike the 1650, it contains an internal
2025-11-30 13:44:42 [gemini-3-pro] controller.
2025-11-30 13:44:42 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-11-30 13:44:42 [gemini-3-pro] • Programming Guide: Yes, the 1670 utilizes a subset of the industry-standard Hayes
2025-11-30 13:44:42 [gemini-3-pro] "AT" command set.
2025-11-30 13:44:42 [gemini-3-pro] • Control Method: While you still use KERNAL routines to open the port, you control the
2025-11-30 13:44:42 [gemini-3-pro] modem's behavior (dialing, answering, hanging up) by sending ASCII string commands (e.g., `ATDT` to
2025-11-30 13:44:42 [gemini-3-pro] dial, `ATH` to hang up) over the data stream.
2025-11-30 13:44:42 [gemini-3-pro] • Documentation: The Commodore 1670 User Manual includes a specific section or appendix
2025-11-30 13:44:42 [gemini-3-pro] listing the supported AT commands and internal S-registers.
