2025-11-30 13:49:36 [gemini-3-pro] Here is the information regarding User Port devices and programming for the Commodore 64.
2025-11-30 13:49:36 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-11-30 13:49:36 [gemini-3-pro] Common User Port Devices
2025-11-30 13:49:36 [gemini-3-pro] • Modems: Commodore 1650 (Automodem), 1660, 1670, and modern Wi-Fi modems (ESP8266/ESP32
2025-11-30 13:49:36 [gemini-3-pro] based).
2025-11-30 13:49:36 [gemini-3-pro] • Interface Adapters: RS-232 level shifters (TTL to RS-232) and Parallel Printer interfaces
2025-11-30 13:49:36 [gemini-3-pro] (e.g., GeoCable).
2025-11-30 13:49:36 [gemini-3-pro] • Controllers: Relay boards, EPROM programmers, and digitizers/samplers (e.g., Covox Voice
2025-11-30 13:49:36 [gemini-3-pro] Master).
2025-11-30 13:49:36 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-11-30 13:49:36 [gemini-3-pro] Programming Guides (1650 & 1670)
2025-11-30 13:49:36 [gemini-3-pro] • 1650: This is a pulse-dialing modem. It does not have an internal command set.
2025-11-30 13:49:36 [gemini-3-pro] Programming requires direct manipulation of the CIA #2 registers for dialing and standard KERNAL
2025-11-30 13:49:36 [gemini-3-pro] RS-232 routines for data transfer. The best reference is the Commodore 64 Programmer's
2025-11-30 13:49:36 [gemini-3-pro] Reference Guide, Chapter 6.
2025-11-30 13:49:36 [gemini-3-pro] • 1670: This is a "smart" modem (1200 baud) that uses a subset of the Hayes AT command set.
2025-11-30 13:49:36 [gemini-3-pro] You can control it by sending string commands (e.g., `ATDT 5551234`) over the RS-232 channel.
2025-11-30 13:49:36 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-11-30 13:49:36 [gemini-3-pro] Baud Rates and Emulation
2025-11-30 13:49:36 [gemini-3-pro] • Fastest KERNAL Rate: The standard C64 KERNAL `OPEN` routines are generally reliable only
2025-11-30 13:49:36 [gemini-3-pro] up to 1200 baud. While 2400 baud is technically selectable, it often results in data loss
2025-11-30 13:49:36 [gemini-3-pro] without custom assembly language drivers (used by software like Novaterm or CCGMS) that bypass the
2025-11-30 13:49:36 [gemini-3-pro] KERNAL.
2025-11-30 13:49:36 [gemini-3-pro] • Emulating 1670: Yes, modern Wi-Fi modems plugged into the User Port generally emulate a
2025-11-30 13:49:36 [gemini-3-pro] Hayes-compatible modem. If you set the Wi-Fi modem to 1200 baud, it effectively acts as a 1670,
2025-11-30 13:49:36 [gemini-3-pro] allowing you to "dial" BBS addresses via Telnet using `ATDT`.
2025-11-30 13:49:36 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-11-30 13:49:36 [gemini-3-pro] BASIC Telnet Terminal (1200 Baud)
2025-11-30 13:49:36 [gemini-3-pro] The following is a minimal terminal program using standard KERNAL I/O. It configures the port for
2025-11-30 13:49:36 [gemini-3-pro] 1200 baud, 8 data bits, no parity, and 1 stop bit.
2025-11-30 13:49:36 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-11-30 13:49:36 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-11-30 13:49:36 [gemini-3-pro] 10 REM *** C64 BASIC TERMINAL ***
2025-11-30 13:49:36 [gemini-3-pro] 20 REM SET 1200 BAUD, 8N1
2025-11-30 13:49:36 [gemini-3-pro] 30 OPEN 2,2,0,CHR$(8)
2025-11-30 13:49:36 [gemini-3-pro] 40 REM CHECK MODEM INPUT
2025-11-30 13:49:36 [gemini-3-pro] 50 GET#2,A$
2025-11-30 13:49:36 [gemini-3-pro] 60 IF A$<>"" THEN PRINT A$;
2025-11-30 13:49:36 [gemini-3-pro] 70 REM CHECK KEYBOARD INPUT
2025-11-30 13:49:36 [gemini-3-pro] 80 GET B$
2025-11-30 13:49:36 [gemini-3-pro] 90 IF B$<>"" THEN PRINT#2,B$;
2025-11-30 13:49:36 [gemini-3-pro] 100 GOTO 40
2025-11-30 13:49:36 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-11-30 13:49:36 [gemini-3-pro] 
2025-11-30 13:49:36 [gemini-3-pro] • Notes on the Control Character (Line 30):*
2025-11-30 13:49:36 [gemini-3-pro] • `CHR$(8)` sets 1200 baud.
2025-11-30 13:49:36 [gemini-3-pro] • `CHR$(6)` sets 300 baud.
2025-11-30 13:49:36 [gemini-3-pro] • If you see "garbage" characters, ensure your Wi-Fi modem is configured to match the baud rate
2025-11-30 13:49:36 [gemini-3-pro] defined in line 30.
2025-11-30 13:49:36 [gemini-3-pro] 
