2025-12-08 22:28:43 [kimi-k2-0905] The labels are now being resolved (`stub` → 0803, `start` → 0805 …) but the bytes that come out are
2025-12-08 22:28:43 [kimi-k2-0905] still wrong.
2025-12-08 22:28:43 [kimi-k2-0905] The reason is that the assembler is never told to change its origin when it meets the
2025-12-08 22:28:43 [kimi-k2-0905] directive
2025-12-08 22:28:43 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-12-08 22:28:43 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-12-08 22:28:43 [kimi-k2-0905] * = $0801
2025-12-08 22:28:43 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-12-08 22:28:43 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-12-08 22:28:43 [kimi-k2-0905] so every `.word` and every instruction is still being written at offset 0 in the buffer instead of
2025-12-08 22:28:43 [kimi-k2-0905] at $0801.
2025-12-08 22:28:43 [kimi-k2-0905] Teach the assembler to relocate its physical write-pointer when it sees the origin directive.
2025-12-08 22:28:43 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-12-08 22:28:43 [kimi-k2-0905] 1. Add a helper that maps the current logical address (`org + pos`) to the physical offset in the
2025-12-08 22:28:43 [kimi-k2-0905] buffer.
2025-12-08 22:28:43 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-12-08 22:28:43 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-12-08 22:28:43 [kimi-k2-0905] private phys(): number {
2025-12-08 22:28:43 [kimi-k2-0905] 	return this.org + this.pos - this.base;
2025-12-08 22:28:43 [kimi-k2-0905] }
2025-12-08 22:28:43 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-12-08 22:28:43 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-12-08 22:28:43 [kimi-k2-0905] 2. Initialise `base` to the first origin you meet (default 0 until you see `* = …`).
2025-12-08 22:28:43 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-12-08 22:28:43 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-12-08 22:28:43 [kimi-k2-0905] private base = 0;
2025-12-08 22:28:43 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-12-08 22:28:43 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-12-08 22:28:43 [kimi-k2-0905] 3. In `add()`, when you see the origin directive update both `org` and—on the first time—`base`,
2025-12-08 22:28:43 [kimi-k2-0905] then reset `pos`.
2025-12-08 22:28:43 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-12-08 22:28:43 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-12-08 22:28:43 [kimi-k2-0905] case '*':
2025-12-08 22:28:43 [kimi-k2-0905] 	// * = $0801
2025-12-08 22:28:43 [kimi-k2-0905] 	const newOrg = parseInt(trim.substring(3), 16);
2025-12-08 22:28:43 [kimi-k2-0905] 	if (this.base === 0) {          // first ORG fixes load address
2025-12-08 22:28:44 [kimi-k2-0905] 		this.base = newOrg;
2025-12-08 22:28:44 [kimi-k2-0905] 	}
2025-12-08 22:28:44 [kimi-k2-0905] 	this.org = newOrg;
2025-12-08 22:28:44 [kimi-k2-0905] 	this.pos = 0;                   // write pointer starts at origin
2025-12-08 22:28:44 [kimi-k2-0905] 	break;
2025-12-08 22:28:44 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-12-08 22:28:44 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-12-08 22:28:44 [kimi-k2-0905] 4. Every place that currently uses `this.pos` must now use `this.phys()` instead, e.g.
2025-12-08 22:28:44 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-12-08 22:28:44 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-12-08 22:28:44 [kimi-k2-0905] poke(byte: number) {
2025-12-08 22:28:44 [kimi-k2-0905] 	this.#bytes[this.phys()] = byte;
2025-12-08 22:28:44 [kimi-k2-0905] 	this.pos++;
2025-12-08 22:28:44 [kimi-k2-0905] }
2025-12-08 22:28:44 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-12-08 22:28:44 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-12-08 22:28:44 [kimi-k2-0905] (The same change applies to `pokeop`, `poke16`, `pokeString`, etc.)
2025-12-08 22:28:44 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-12-08 22:28:44 [kimi-k2-0905] After these four steps the assembler will place the BASIC header at 0801, the code at 0805, and the
2025-12-08 22:28:44 [kimi-k2-0905] second pass will overwrite the `.word` slots with the correct addresses.
2025-12-08 22:28:44 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-12-08 22:30:28 [simon@midnightblue] org is at 0x801 bytes is offset from org
2025-12-08 22:30:44 [kimi-k2-0905] Then just treat the buffer as starting at 0x0801 and keep the write pointer (`pos`) as an
2025-12-08 22:30:44 [kimi-k2-0905] absolute address.
2025-12-08 22:30:44 [kimi-k2-0905] No extra `base` is needed—everything becomes a one-liner.
2025-12-08 22:30:44 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-12-08 22:30:44 [kimi-k2-0905] 1. Initialise once:
2025-12-08 22:30:44 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-12-08 22:30:44 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-12-08 22:30:44 [kimi-k2-0905] org  = 0x0801;
2025-12-08 22:30:44 [kimi-k2-0905] pos  = 0x0801;      // absolute address we are about to write
2025-12-08 22:30:44 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-12-08 22:30:44 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-12-08 22:30:44 [kimi-k2-0905] 2. When the source says `* = $xxxx` simply set both variables:
2025-12-08 22:30:44 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-12-08 22:30:44 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-12-08 22:30:44 [kimi-k2-0905] case '*':
2025-12-08 22:30:44 [kimi-k2-0905] 	this.org = parseInt(trim.substring(3), 16);
2025-12-08 22:30:44 [kimi-k2-0905] 	this.pos = this.org;
2025-12-08 22:30:44 [kimi-k2-0905] 	break;
2025-12-08 22:30:44 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-12-08 22:30:44 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-12-08 22:30:44 [kimi-k2-0905] 3. Every physical write uses `pos - 0x0801` as the index:
2025-12-08 22:30:44 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-12-08 22:30:44 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-12-08 22:30:44 [kimi-k2-0905] poke(b: number) {
2025-12-08 22:30:44 [kimi-k2-0905] 	this.#bytes[this.pos - 0x0801] = b;
2025-12-08 22:30:44 [kimi-k2-0905] 	this.pos++;
2025-12-08 22:30:44 [kimi-k2-0905] }
2025-12-08 22:30:44 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-12-08 22:30:44 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-12-08 22:30:44 [kimi-k2-0905] Same for `poke16`, `pokeString`, etc.
2025-12-08 22:30:44 [kimi-k2-0905] The label values you record in pass 0 are already absolute addresses, so in pass 1 `.word stub`
2025-12-08 22:30:44 [kimi-k2-0905] will drop the correct 16-bit value into the slot that was reserved at the proper offset.
2025-12-08 22:30:44 [kimi-k2-0905] 
