2025-12-08 22:42:15 [simon@midnightblue] [PET] [@$801] Uint8Array(29) [    3,   8,   0,   0, 162,   0, 161,  19,    8, 240, 244,  32, 210, 255, 232, 208,  244,  96, 104, 101, 108, 108, 111,  32,  115, 116, 117,  13,   0]
2025-12-08 22:42:22 [kimi-k2-0905] Clean 29-byte image—exactly one copy of the code and string.
2025-12-08 22:42:22 [kimi-k2-0905] The first two bytes (`03 08`) are the correct little-endian link address for a BASIC line that
2025-12-08 22:42:22 [kimi-k2-0905] starts at $0803, so the 6502 payload will be launched by `SYS 2061`.
2025-12-08 22:42:22 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-12-08 22:48:00 [simon@midnightblue] sys 2061 just clears the screen
2025-12-08 22:48:15 [kimi-k2-0905] `SYS 2061` lands at $0803, the first byte after the BASIC header.
2025-12-08 22:48:15 [kimi-k2-0905] Your first three instructions are:
2025-12-08 22:48:15 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-12-08 22:48:15 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-12-08 22:48:15 [kimi-k2-0905] $0803  A2 00     LDX #$00
2025-12-08 22:48:15 [kimi-k2-0905] $0805  A1 00     LDA ($00,X)
2025-12-08 22:48:15 [kimi-k2-0905] $0807  08        PHP
2025-12-08 22:48:15 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-12-08 22:48:15 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-12-08 22:48:15 [kimi-k2-0905] Whatever is at zero-page address 00/01 (the BASIC “start-of-text” pointer) is being pulled into A
2025-12-08 22:48:15 [kimi-k2-0905] and then the flags are pushed.
2025-12-08 22:48:15 [kimi-k2-0905] If that value happens to be 93 (the PETSCII “clear-screen” character) the KERNAL’s standard output
2025-12-08 22:48:15 [kimi-k2-0905] routine at FFD2 will clear the screen when it eventually sees that byte.
2025-12-08 22:48:15 [kimi-k2-0905] The code then falls straight into the RTS at $0812, so the machine returns to BASIC having done
2025-12-08 22:48:15 [kimi-k2-0905] nothing visibly useful.
2025-12-08 22:48:15 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-12-08 22:48:15 [kimi-k2-0905] In short: the program is running, but it is only reading one byte and finishing; the “clear” you
2025-12-08 22:48:15 [kimi-k2-0905] see is a side-effect of whatever byte was already in memory.
2025-12-08 22:48:15 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-12-08 22:49:57 [kimi-k2-0905] Nothing new—just the same fix we already discussed (`pet.begin(pass)` zeroing `pos` each pass).
2025-12-08 22:49:57 [kimi-k2-0905] 
