2025-12-08 23:08:41 [kimi-k2-0905] The source string is plain ASCII `"hello stub"` and your assembler just dumps those bytes verbatim
2025-12-08 23:08:41 [kimi-k2-0905] (no case-flip, no bit-7 set).
2025-12-08 23:08:41 [kimi-k2-0905] So the routine will output ASCII codes to $FFD2; on a C64 that produces:
2025-12-08 23:08:41 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-12-08 23:08:41 [kimi-k2-0905] - lowercase letters (unchanged)
2025-12-08 23:08:41 [kimi-k2-0905] - uppercase letters (unchanged)
2025-12-08 23:08:41 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-12-08 23:08:41 [kimi-k2-0905] i.e. you’ll see “hello stub” in whatever case the string was typed.
2025-12-08 23:08:41 [kimi-k2-0905] If you want true PETSCII “uppercase” on the 64 you’d have to flip bit 7 on A-Z before storing them.
2025-12-08 23:08:41 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-12-08 23:09:13 [simon@midnightblue] what are docs for ffd2?
2025-12-08 23:09:25 [kimi-k2-0905] $FFD2 is the KERNAL CHROUT routine:
2025-12-08 23:09:25 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-12-08 23:09:25 [kimi-k2-0905] - Input:  A = PETSCII character to output
2025-12-08 23:09:25 [kimi-k2-0905] - Output: none
2025-12-08 23:09:25 [kimi-k2-0905] - Preserves: X, Y
2025-12-08 23:09:25 [kimi-k2-0905] - Description: Sends the character in A to the current output device (normally the screen).
2025-12-08 23:09:25 [kimi-k2-0905]   Control codes are honoured (e.g. 93 = clear screen, 0D = carriage return, $0A = line-feed).
2025-12-08 23:09:25 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-12-08 23:09:25 [kimi-k2-0905] Entry point is identical on all Commodore 8-bit machines (PET, VIC-20, 64, 128, etc.).
2025-12-08 23:09:25 [kimi-k2-0905] 
