2025-12-08 23:17:10 [kimi-k2-0905] The PRG’s BASIC header ends at $080C, so the file is loaded at:
2025-12-08 23:17:10 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-12-08 23:17:10 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-12-08 23:17:10 [kimi-k2-0905] $0801  03 08  next-line ptr
2025-12-08 23:17:10 [kimi-k2-0905] $0803  00 08  line-# 2064
2025-12-08 23:17:10 [kimi-k2-0905] $0805  9E     token SYS
2025-12-08 23:17:10 [kimi-k2-0905] $0806  32 30 36 31  ASCII "2061"
2025-12-08 23:17:10 [kimi-k2-0905] $080B  00     end-of-line
2025-12-08 23:17:10 [kimi-k2-0905] $080C  00 00  end-of-program
2025-12-08 23:17:10 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-12-08 23:17:10 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-12-08 23:17:10 [kimi-k2-0905] The machine-language routine therefore starts at $080C, and the BASIC line says
2025-12-08 23:17:10 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-12-08 23:17:10 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-12-08 23:17:10 [kimi-k2-0905] 10 SYS 2061
2025-12-08 23:17:10 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-12-08 23:17:10 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-12-08 23:17:10 [kimi-k2-0905] 2061 decimal = 080D, so BASIC will JSR 080D—one byte into your code.
2025-12-08 23:17:10 [kimi-k2-0905] That is exactly what you want: the first instruction (`LDX #00`) is at 080D, so the entry point is
2025-12-08 23:17:10 [kimi-k2-0905] correct.
