2025-12-14 22:28:18 [simon@midnightblue] how does c64 cursor work and inverse space?
2025-12-14 22:28:36 [kimi-k2-0905] The VIC-II chip has no hardware cursor.
2025-12-14 22:28:36 [kimi-k2-0905] What you see is a 1-byte “cursor register” in the C-64 KERNAL that the screen-editor IRQ routine
2025-12-14 22:28:36 [kimi-k2-0905] uses as a character code to XOR-blink over whatever is already on the screen.
2025-12-14 22:28:36 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-12-14 22:28:36 [kimi-k2-0905] - Location 204, `$CC` – “cursor character”
2025-12-14 22:28:36 [kimi-k2-0905] - Location 211, `$D3` – “cursor blink phase” (0 = off, 1 = on)
2025-12-14 22:28:36 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-12-14 22:28:36 [kimi-k2-0905] Every 1/60 s the IRQ does:
2025-12-14 22:28:36 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-12-14 22:28:36 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-12-14 22:28:36 [kimi-k2-0905] lda $D3          ; 0 or 1
2025-12-14 22:28:36 [kimi-k2-0905] beq cursor_off
2025-12-14 22:28:36 [kimi-k2-0905] lda $CC          ; usually $A0 (space)
2025-12-14 22:28:36 [kimi-k2-0905] eor ($FB),y      ; XOR with screen byte
2025-12-14 22:28:36 [kimi-k2-0905] sta ($FB),y
2025-12-14 22:28:36 [kimi-k2-0905] cursor_off: …
2025-12-14 22:28:36 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-12-14 22:28:36 [kimi-k2-0905] 
2025-12-14 22:28:36 [kimi-k2-0905] `A0` (space) XORed with the background colour becomes inverse space, so the KERNAL initialises `CC`
2025-12-14 22:28:36 [kimi-k2-0905] to `$A0`.
2025-12-14 22:28:36 [kimi-k2-0905] If you POKE 204,1 you get an inverse-A, POKE 204,65 you get flashing “A”, etc.
2025-12-14 22:28:36 [kimi-k2-0905] No extra sprite, no VIC cursor register—just one byte and an XOR in software.
2025-12-14 22:28:36 [kimi-k2-0905] 